"Passage 1:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nTo naturally meet you in the caf\u00e9, Mr. Poirot... Harriet can't pull that off. Richard and Ada can't either. So you see, I must live to the end. [1899] But the plans that Harriet and Ada knew were not Richard's true plans. Two weeks after meeting with Harriet and Ada, Richard came to London to see me alone, at which time he told me that the question of whether Nancy should die had been weighing heavily on him. He shared my belief that Nancy had not confessed to Harriet about the conversation and that there was no other reason for her to confess at the White Hart Inn other than to clear Patrick's name from the rumors. [1900] On the other hand, Richard also understood what Harriet meant. The death of Patrick and Francis Eve was caused by the misjudgment of multiple people, and Nancy was also not blameless. [1901] When Richard admitted that he couldn't make a decision about Nancy and decided to leave it up to me, I was shocked and terrified beyond measure. He said that after Harriet, Ada, and he were dead, I could choose whether to try my best to have Nancy hanged or commit suicide and leave a completely different note. Not one saying \"May they rest in peace,\" but one revealing the truth of the death. [1902] I begged Richard not to let me make the decision alone. I kept asking him, \"Why choose me?\". [1903] \"Because, Jenny,\" he said, and I'll never forget what he said next, \"because you are the best among us. And you never let it go to your head. Yes, you've lied, but as soon as you said it, you realized you were wrong. I've believed your lies for too long, and that's unforgivable. I've fueled the fire, supporting everyone to accuse an innocent good man. Yes, he had his faults, but who among us is perfect?\" [1904] \"Well,\" I told Richard, \"I'll make the decision and fulfill your wish.\" [1905] And so, our plan was set. Now, do you want to know how we managed to mess everything up? [1906] Chapter Twenty\n[1907] How We Screwed It All Up\n[1908] \"Indeed, we'd like to know,\" said Poirot. \"Both Casterpoole and I are curious.\" [1909] \"It's all my fault,\" said Jenny's voice, hoarse. \"I'm a coward, I really fear death. Without Patrick, my life was miserable, but I got used to the pain and didn't want to end it. Any kind of life, even filled with torment, is better than death! Please don't condemn me for violating Christian teachings, but I truly don't believe in an afterlife. As the day of execution drew nearer, I grew increasingly afraid, afraid to kill. I imagined what it would be like: standing in a locked room, watching Richard drink the poison, and I didn't want to do it. But I agreed! I promised.\" [1910] \"A few months ago, the plan seemed so easy to implement, now it seems so difficult,\" said Poirot. \"Of course, you couldn't tell Richard Nielsen how much you feared it, as he held you in high regard. If you admitted your fear, he might have looked down on you. Perhaps you were also worried that he might kill you regardless of your consent.\" [1911] \"Yes! I'm really afraid he'll do that. You see, from our conversations, I realized how important it is for him that the four of us die. He once told me that if Harry and Ada couldn't be convinced, he would 'do what he had to do without their consent.' That's what he said. He's so decisive, how can I tell him I've changed my mind and don't want to die or kill?\" [1912] \"I suppose you feel guilty about it too, ma'am. You also think that this murder and death are honorable and right, don't you?\" [1913] \"Rationally, yes, I do think so,\" said Jane. \"I hope and pray that I can find the courage to complete this task.\" [1914] \"How do you plan to deal with Nancy Duane?\" I asked Jane. [1915] \"I don't know. The panic from our first meeting that night has not subsided, Mr. Poirot. I haven't made any decisions! I let Semmi continue to fabricate the story about the key and to point the finger at Nancy. I let things take their natural course and tell myself that I can always go to the authorities and save her. But... I didn't do that. Richard thinks I'm better suited to do this, but he's wrong\u2014dead wrong.\" [1916] \"I still envy Nancy because Patrick loves her, and that was the root of the disaster at Grelling's. And... I know that if I admit to conspiring to frame an innocent woman, I'll go to jail. I'm scared.\" [1917] \"Lady, tell us, what did you do? On that day... the day of the 'execution', what did you do at the Browns Hotel? \" [1918] \"I was supposed to be there at six, the time we had agreed to meet.\" [1919] \"Do you mean the four conspirators?\" [1920] \"Yes, and Semmy. All day, I watched the clock tick, waiting for that terrible moment to come. At five o'clock, I realized I couldn't do it. I couldn't! So I didn't go to the hotel. I ran through the streets of London in fear, not knowing where to go or what to do, just running. I thought Richard Nichol would come looking for me, and he would be angry because I disappointed him and everyone else. I went to 'The Happy Cafe', thinking that since I couldn't kill Richard as promised, at least I could complete this part.\" [1921] \"When I got to the cafe, I started to worry about my life. Of course, nothing happened, you saw it yourself. I thought Richard, not Nancy, would kill me. What was even more terrifying was that I believed that if he killed me, he would be doing the right thing. I deserved to die! I haven't told you a single lie, M. Poirot, please recall what I said. I was afraid to be killed? Yes, I was afraid of Richard coming to kill me. Have I done terrible things in the past? Yes. And if Richard had really caught and killed me, I believed it was only a matter of time, and I didn't want him to be punished. I know I disappointed him. Can you understand that? Richard may have been seeking death, but I wanted him to live. Although he had hurt Patrick, he was a good man.\" [1922] \"Yes, Madam.\" [1923] \"M. Poirot, that night, I wanted to tell you the truth, but I didn't have the courage.\" [1924] \"So, you believe that Richard Nigges would have found you and killed you because you didn't go to Brocks Hotel to kill him that day?\" [1925] \"Yes. I think he wouldn't have been satisfied with dying without knowing why I didn't show up at the hotel.\" [1926] \"Indeed.\" I said, seething with anger. [1927] Jane nodded. [1928] I finally understood why the scene was as it was: the three bodies were in identical positions, between the small coffee table and the chairs, upright with feet facing the door. As Mr. Poirot said, it was impossible for Harriet Spear, Ada Gransbury, and Richard Nixes to all end up in that position. [1929] The similarities between the three murder scenes were suspicious, and now I understood why: the conspirators wanted the police to believe there was only one killer. On the same night, at the same hotel, three bodies, with a cufflink in each mouth. Any competent detective could have deduced this was the work of one person, and an obsessive one at that. But they knew there were more than one killer, so they were afraid, afraid of the truth being revealed, so they went to great lengths to make the three murder scenes look very similar, even overdoing it. [1930] The bodies lying neatly on the ground, upright and identical, also aligned with their intentions: what happened at Browns Hotel wasn't a murder, but an execution. After each execution, there were subsequent procedures, like a religious ritual. I thought it was better to arrange the bodies properly than to leave them lying around, not looking like the work of an ordinary killer or a garden killer. [1931] Suddenly, a younger Jane Hobbs appeared in my mind, tidying up the beds in the rooms at Jesus College, Cambridge, one by one. She would arrange each bed in the same pattern...\n[1932] Beds, death beds...\n[1933] Pattern, disrupted pattern...\n[1934] \"Richard Naylegh committed suicide.\" I blurted out involuntarily, \"It was suicide. He wanted it to look like murder, so he followed the pattern of the other two, he wanted us to think it was the same killer, but he had to lock the door from the inside. That's why he hid the key behind the tiles in the fireplace, pretending that the killer took the key and then escaped through the window. If the key was found, we would have been suspicious - and we were indeed puzzled - why the killer locked the door from the inside and hid the key in the room, yet escaped through the window. But we would still believe there was only one killer. Naylegh only cared about that. But if the window was closed and the key was found, we would only conclude that Richard Naylegh committed suicide. He would not take the risk of letting us draw that conclusion, right? Once we came to that conclusion, the plot to frame Nancy Duane for the murders of three people would fail, and we would be more likely to believe that Naylegh first killed Harriet Spear and Ada Gransbury and then committed suicide.\" [1935] \"Yes,\" Jane said, \"I think you're right.\" [1936] \"The position of the cufflinks is different...\" murmured Poirot, raising his eyebrows at me to continue. [1937] \"The cufflinks were so close to Nigges' throat because he convulsed after taking the poison, causing his mouth to open. He had to be careful to swallow the poison while keeping his body straight, placing the cufflinks between his lips, but they slipped into his mouth. Unlike Harriet Sipper and Ada Gransberry, Richard Nigges died without anyone helping him arrange the scene, so the cufflinks couldn't be placed in the same position as planned.\" [1938] \"Do you think Mr. Nigges would have taken the poison, not knowing why you weren't at the hotel?\" Poirot asked Jenny. [1939] \"I never believed it until I read the news of his death in the paper.\" [1940] \"Ah.\" Poirot's expression was inscrutable. [1941] \"For a long time, Richard had been looking forward to ending his life on Thursday night, putting an end to years of guilt and torment.\" Mrs. Vane said, \"I believe he went to the Blaxland Hotel to die, so even if I hadn't shown up to kill him, he had completed his self-inflicted death.\" [1942] \"Thank you, madam.\" Poirot stood up, almost losing his balance due to sitting for too long. [1943] \"What will you do with me, Mr. Poirot?\" [1944] \"Please stay at home until I or Mr. Cattermole visit again. If you run away again, it will be even worse for you.\" [1945] \"Even if I stay here, things won't get better.\" Mrs. Vane said, her eyes filled with despair and emptiness. \"Yes, Mr. Cattermole, you shouldn't worry about me. I'm prepared for what's to come.\" [1946] Without a doubt, she said this to reassure me, but it made me feel fear. Her attitude seemed to see the future, to see how terrible things would happen. But whatever happens, I know I'm not ready and don't want to be ready. [1947] Chapter 21\n[1948] All demons\n[1949] All day long, Poirot didn't speak, except for repeating twice that we must go to Grelling immediately and not waste any time. He looked preoccupied and obviously didn't want to talk. [1950] We returned to our residence and saw Stanley Bill waiting for us. Poirot asked, \"What's the matter? You've come because of my artwork, haven't you?\" [1951] \"What, sir? Oh, your artwork? No, that's fine, sir. Actually\u2026\" Bill took an envelope out of his pocket and handed it to Poirot, \"This has the answer you wanted.\" [1952] \"Thank you, officer. But there must be something, right? You look anxious. Is it about that?\" [1953] \"Yes, sir. I came to deliver a message. A man named Ambrose Flavard went to Scotland Yard to see Mr. Cargraves. He said he's the doctor from Gregholing village and needs to see him urgently, saying he can help.\" [1954] Poirot looked at me and then back at Stanley Bill, saying, \"We were just about to go there. Do you know why he wants to see Cargraves?\" [1955] \"I'm afraid I do, sir. It's not good news, sir. A woman named Margaret Ernst was attacked and is dying\u2026\"\n[1956] \"Oh, no.\" I muttered. [1957] \"And she wants to see Mr. Cader before she dies. Sir, considering what Dr. Flavard said, I suggest you hurry. The car is outside and can take you to the station.\" [1958] Considering Poirot's methodical and dislike of chaos, I said, \"Can we give us half an hour to prepare?\" [1959] Bill looked at his watch and said again, \"Sir, we have at most five to ten minutes, we can't waste time, otherwise, you'll miss the next train.\" [1960] I had to admit, this time Poirot was faster than me, he had already packed his luggage and was hurrying me downstairs, saying, \"Hurry up, my friend.\" [1961] In the car, I decided not to remain silent, even though I knew Poirot didn't want to talk. \"If I didn't go to that damn village, Margaret Ernst wouldn't have been attacked.\" I said coldly, \"Someone must have seen me go to her house and stay there for a long time.\" [1962] \"Long enough for her to tell you everything, or almost everything. But since she has already told the police everything, what's the point of killing her?\" [1963] \"Revenge, punishment. Honestly, it doesn't make sense. If Nancy Duane is innocent, then Jenny Hobbs and Samuel Kid are the masterminds behind it - I mean the living masterminds, then why would Jenny and Kid kill Margaret Ernst? Margaret's words won't threaten them, and she never harmed Patrick and Francis Ives.\" [1964] \"I agree with your point of view. I also don't think Jenny Hobbs and Samuel Kid want to kill Margaret Ernst.\" [1965] The rain pattered on the car window, making it difficult for us to hear each other and concentrate. I asked again, \"Who could it be? I thought all the problems were solved -\"\n[1966] \"Carter, you can't really believe that, can you?\" [1967] \"Yes, I do. I guess you'll say I'm wrong, but didn't all the problems get solved? Before we knew Margaret Ernst was attacked, everything was clear.\" [1968] \"You actually think everything is clear!\" Poirot looked out the window at the rain pounding against the glass, chuckling foolishly. [1969] \"Well, to me, it's quite clear. All the killers are dead. Edith killed Harry with her consent, and Richard Nix killed her willingly. Since Jeanne didn't kill Nix as planned, he chose to commit suicide. Jeanne Hobbs and Samuel Kid didn't kill anyone. Of course, they were involved in the murder, but I think it's not really a murder, but \u2013\"\n[1970] \"Voluntarily sentenced to death?\" [1971] \"Exactly.\" [1972] \"What an ingenious plan, isn't it? Harriet Sipper, Ada Gransbury, Richard Nigges, and Jenny Hobbs, let's call them A, B, C, and D, and see how clever their plan was.\" [1973] \"Why not use their names?\" I asked. [1974] Poirot didn't answer me. \"A, B, C, and D, all four had guilt and sought redemption for their souls. They all agreed that they would use their lives to make up for the sins they had committed, so they planned to kill each other: B killed A, then C killed B, and finally D killed C.\"\n[1975] \"But D didn't kill C, did she? D represents Jenny Hobbs, who didn't kill Richard Nigges.\" [1976] \"Perhaps, but the plan was for her to do so. Moreover, D must live long enough to see E \u2013 Nancy Drew, who was hanged for murdering A, B, and C. By then, D\u2026\" He suddenly stopped, said \"D\" again, and then said, \"Demise, that's the correct answer.\" [1977] \"What?\" [1978] \"Your crossword puzzle. A six-letter word meaning 'death', do you remember? I said the answer was 'murder', you said no, unless 'murder' began with\u2026\" He stopped again, shaking his head. [1979] \"If 'murder' had started with D, that would have been perfect. Yes, I remember. Poirot, are you all right? His eyes glinted with strange green light, which occasionally occurred. [1980] \"What? Of course! If 'murder' had started with D, that would have been perfect! Yes! That's it! My friend, you've done me a great favor! Now I believe\u2026yes, that's the truth. The truth must be like this. The old couple \u2013 ah, everything is clear now!\" [1981] \"Please explain what's going on.\" [1982] \"Okay, okay, but I need to prepare myself first.\" [1983] \"What's not ready? What are you waiting for?\" [1984] \"Kattakur, you at least need to give me twenty seconds to collect my thoughts and organize my language. I need time to explain to someone who's completely in the dark, and I see you haven't understood anything yet. You say everything is clear, but the story Jenny Hobbs told us this morning was a carefully crafted lie! Don't you see that?\" [1985] \"Eh... Do you mean... \"\n[1986] \"Harriet Spear wants to pin three murders on Nancy Drew, making her hang, but would Richard Nixes really agree to that? And would he leave Nancy's fate in the hands of Jenny Hobbs? This leader, Richard Nixes, a beloved authority figure who has been guilty for sixteen years for unjustly attacking Patrick Eves, would he do that?\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHowever, while working with Nancy, Jenny pretended to be involved in another plot, making Richard Nix believe she was his accomplice.\" [2382] \"That doesn't sound 'simple' to me.\" I said, \"It's quite complex.\" [2383] \"No, no, friend, it's not complicated at all. Your problem is that you've mixed different versions of this story together. You have to forget everything Jenny said at Samuel Kid's house and erase it from your mind completely. She was lying from beginning to end, of course, I don't deny there was some truth in it. The most convincing lies have a grain of truth. Soon, Jenny will tell us the whole truth, she's not afraid of anything now. But first, my friend, I must commend you, it was your clue at the Saint's Churchyard that made me understand everything.\" [2384] Poirot turned to Jenny and said, \"You told Harriet Westphal that Patrick Yves was helping parishioners communicate with their deceased loved ones for money, and that Nancy Duane often went to the vicar's house at night to talk to her deceased husband William. How many times have I heard this terrible, wicked rumor? Many, many times. Miss Hobbs, you admitted to us that day that you said these lies out of jealousy and weakness. But that's not true!\" [2385] \"Standing in front of the desecrated grave of Patrick and Francis Yves, Kitcher told me, 'If Jenny Hobbs made up lies not to hurt Patrick Yves, but to help him?' Kitcher realized a key point that I took for granted. This has never been suspected, so I never verified it: that Harriet Westphal was crazy about her late husband George. Didn't Poirot know how much Harriet loved George? Didn't he also know how George's death turned Harriet from a happy, kind woman into a bitter, evil demon? It's hard for an outsider to imagine such a terrible, destructive disaster that could wipe out all joy and destroy all virtues. Yes, of course, I know Harriet Westphal has experienced such a disaster. I believe it, which made me not doubt! [2386] \"I also know that Jenny Hobbs loves Patrick Yves and is willing to sacrifice her own happiness and abandon her fianc\u00e9 Samuel Kid to continue serving the Yves couple. It's a selfless love, willing to pay without expecting anything in return. But in the story Jenny and Nancy told us, they said that Jenny lied out of jealousy, jealous of the love Patrick gave to Nancy. That's not possible! It's inconsistent! We must consider both feasibility and psychological factors. Patrick Yves married Francis without punishment from Jenny, accepting the reality that he belonged to another woman. She continued to be his faithful servant in the vicarage, helping him and his wife, and the couple treated her well. For years, Jenny had selflessly loved and served Patrick, so why did his love for Nancy suddenly drive her to lie and cause a series of events that ultimately destroyed him? The answer is, such a thing is impossible.\" [2387] \"It's not jealousy or longing that drove Jenny Hobbs to lie. It's a completely different reason. Miss Hobbs, you've been helping the man you love, haven't you? You could say, to save him. When my clever friend Kitteral said his theory, I realized what the truth was. It's so obvious, Poirot was so dull that he didn't see it!\" [2388] Jenny looked at me and asked, \"What theory?\" [2389] I opened my mouth to explain, but Poirot interrupted, \"When Harriet Cypers told you she saw Nancy Duane visiting the vicarage at night, you immediately sensed danger. You knew about their rendezvous \u2013 you lived in the vicarage, how could you not know? You were eager to protect Patrick Yves' good name. How could you protect his reputation? How could you explain Nancy Duane's late-night visits when Francis Yves was not at home? How could you fool the gossips? Just when you were about to give up hope, it seemed like magic, you suddenly thought of a way. You decided to use temptation and fantasy to eliminate Harriet's threat.\" [2390] Jenny said nothing, staring blankly into the distance. [2391] \"Miss Hariot Westcliff and Nancy Duane have one thing in common,\" Poirot continued. \"Their husbands died young. You told Miss Hariot Westcliff that Patrick Eves could help Nancy communicate with her late husband, William Duane, of course for a fee. Naturally, such a thing had to be kept secret and not revealed to the church or anyone in the village. But you hinted to Miss Hariot Westcliff that if she wanted, Patrick could also help her, just like Nancy, communicate with her husband's spirit. And she and George might\u2026 well, even if they couldn't be together again, at least they could communicate in some way. Tell me, what was her reaction when you told her this?\" [2392] There was a long silence before Jenny said, \"She was salivating, eager to communicate with her late husband. She said she would pay any amount of money to talk to George again. Mr. Poirot, you can't imagine how much she loved that man. I watched her face\u2026 it was like a dead woman coming back to life. I tried to explain the whole thing to Patrick, to tell him what had happened, but I had solved it. You see, I made a promise to Miss Hariot Westcliff without his consent. I knew Patrick wouldn't agree to this, but I had no other choice! I didn't want him to stop me. Can you understand that?\" [2393] \"I understand, madam.\" [2394] \"I hope I can convince him. He is a man of principles, but I know he wants to protect Frances and doesn't want this scandal to hurt her, and he also wants to protect Nancy. And this is the only way to keep Miss Hariot Westcliff silent, the only way! Patrick only had to occasionally comfort Miss Hariot Westcliff with some words, pretending they were from George Westcliff. And he didn't need to ask her for money. I told him all this, but he wouldn't listen. He found it terrifying.\" [2395] \"He would naturally feel that way.\" Poirot said softly. \"Please continue.\" [2396] \"He said that doing as I suggested would be unfair and immoral to Hattie, and would make him more likely to be destroyed. I begged him to reconsider. What harm could there be in making Hattie happy? But Patrick was firm, insisting that I tell Hattie that he couldn't do it. His instructions were clear: 'Jane, don't tell her you lied, or she'll suspect the whole thing is untrue.' He wanted me to tell Hattie that her wishes couldn't be fulfilled.\" [2397] \"And you had to tell her that, with no other choice,\" I said. [2398] \"There was truly no other choice. I told Hattie that Patrick had refused her request, and she immediately turned against him and spread my lies throughout the village. Actually, Patrick could have ruined her reputation, saying she had always wanted him to provide unethical religious services and that if he refused, she would defame him for desecrating God and violating doctrine. But he didn't do that. He said that no matter how maliciously Hattie attacked him, he wouldn't defame her reputation. How foolish! He could have made her shut up immediately, but his morals were too high, and he didn't do that!\" [2399] \"Was it at this point that you went to seek Nancy Drew's advice?\" [2400] \"Yes. I felt it shouldn't only be me and Patrick who were troubled. Nancy was also involved in this. I asked her if I should publicly admit that I had lied, but she advised me not to. She said, 'Jane, I'm worried that doing so will bring trouble to Patrick and me. Jane, the wisest thing to do is to retreat into the background, say nothing, and not sacrifice yourself. I'm not sure if you're strong enough to withstand Hattie's defamation.' She underestimated me. I was very unsettled at the time, and perhaps I looked on the verge of collapse, as I was too worried about Patrick and worried that Hattie would ruin him. But Mr. Poirot, I am not a weak person.\" [2401] \"I know you're not afraid.\" [2402] \"I'm not afraid. The news of Harry Sipper's death gave me a new sense of purpose. The killer did the world a favor by getting rid of that despicable hypocrite.\" [2403] \"Your words bring us back to the question: who killed Harry Sipper? You told us it was Edith Gransby, but that was a lie.\" [2404] \"I don't need to tell you the truth, Poirot sir, since you know it as well as I do.\" [2405] \"Then please have some pity on Mr. Cattermole, who still doesn't understand the whole situation.\" [2406] \"It would be best if you tell him, yes?\" Jane smiled, a weary smile. Suddenly, she seemed more ethereal to me, as if she might vanish at any moment. [2407] \"Very well,\" said Poirot. \"I'll start with Harriet Sipper and Edith Gransberry, two stubborn women who thought themselves just, pushing a good man to an early grave. Did they feel remorse for the deceased? No, on the contrary, they opposed burying him in a consecrated grave. After Richard Nigel's patient persuasion, did these two women regret treating Patrick Ives so badly? Of course not, they wouldn't. It was then, Jane, that I knew you were lying. The truth lies in your story.\" [2408] Jane shrugged and said, \"Anything is possible.\" [2409] \"No, only the truth is possible. I know that Harriet Sipper and Edith Gransberry would never willingly take their own lives. Therefore, they were killed by someone else. To say they committed suicide is easy! You think that if Poirot knew all the dead were willingly taking their own lives, his gray brain cells wouldn't work. It was their chance to repent! What an extraordinary, imaginative story, one that people would believe without question, who would suspect it was fabricated?\" [2410] \"It's my safety measure, to be used only when necessary,\" said Jane. \"I hope you never find me, but I'm afraid you will.\" [2411] \"If I find you, you will need an alibi from seven fifteen to eight ten to protect yourself, and Nancy Drew as well. You and Samuel Kid would only be accused of framing an innocent woman, but not of murder or conspiracy to murder. That's a clever move, admitting to doing wrong to avoid a more serious charge. You eliminated your enemies, and no one would suffer for it, as long as we believed your story \u2013 that Edith Garnsey killed Harriet Spear, Richard Nigger killed Edith Garnsey, and then committed suicide. Lady, your plan was brilliant, but it still outsmarts Hercule Poirot!\" [2412] \"Richard wanted to die.\" Jane said angrily, \"He was not killed, he was seeking death.\" [2413] \"Yes.\" Poirot said, \"That is the only truth in this whole lie.\" [2414] \"It was all his fault. I would not have killed anyone if it weren't for Richard.\" [2415] \"But you did kill, and not just once. It was Cacophony's careless remark that led me to the clue.\" [2416] \"What did he say?\" asked Jane. [2417] \"He said, if the murder started with D\u2026\"\n[2418] Hearing Poirot thank me made me feel a little uneasy. I didn't understand why those few casual words were so important. [2419] Poirot began to speak again: \"Lady, after hearing your story and leaving Samuel Kidd's house, we naturally discussed what you said: the plan you made with Richard Nigges\u2026 Allow me to say that this plan is quite attractive. Simple and clean, like a line of dominoes. But after thinking about it again, I realized that the order of events had changed. It wasn't D down, then C, then B, and finally A; it was B who knocked A down, and then C knocked B down\u2026 But that's not the point.\" [2420] What was he talking about? Jane seemed to have understood it even less. [2421] \"Ah, I must explain it more clearly,\" Poirot said. \"Lady, to make it easier to imagine the order of the murder, I used letters to replace the names. In the story you told us at Samuel Kid's house, the plan was that B killed A, then C killed B, then D killed C, and then D waited for E to be condemned and hanged for murdering A, B, and C, and finally D committed suicide. That is, in your story, you are the D. Understand, Miss Hobbs?\" [2422] Jenny nodded. [2423] \"Good. Mr. Cattermole is a crossword puzzle enthusiast, and at that moment, his interests coincided with mine, and he asked me to think of a word with six letters that meant 'death'. I said 'murder'. Mr. Cattermole said it was not. He said, 'If \"murder\" starts with D, it fits in.' Later, I remembered his words and dared to guess what would happen if the murder really started with D? If the first murderer was not Adela Gransbury, but you, Miss Hobbs?\" [2424] \"As time went on, I came to the conclusion that this was the case. I also knew why you definitely killed Harriet Sipper. From Grilling to the Bluxton Hotel, Harriet and Adela neither took the same train nor the same car, so they didn't know each other's existence, which means there was no such a plan of one killing the other. It was all lies.\" [2425] \"What is the truth?\" I asked eagerly. [2426] \"Harriet Sipper and Edith Garnet both thought they were going to London alone for personal reasons. Harriet was called by Jane, who said she urgently needed to see her and asked for absolute secrecy. Jane told Harriet that she had booked a room for her at the Bluxton Hotel and that she would meet her between 3:30 and 4:00 pm on Thursday to discuss important matters. Harriet accepted Jane's invitation because the reasons mentioned in the invitation letter were irresistible. [2427] \"You offered her something that Patrick Ivor had refused to give her years ago, didn't you, Miss? Communication with her late husband. You told her that her husband George Sipper wanted to communicate with her through you, that he had tried sixteen years ago but failed. Now, George wants to send a message to his beloved wife through you, the medium. He has spoken to you from the other side! Oh, I believe you made a convincing case! Harriet could not resist the temptation and would believe you because she desperately wanted it to be true. She believed the lie you told her years ago that spirits could communicate with the living people they loved, and she never doubted it. [2428] \"You are a clever old man, M. Poirot,\" said Jane. \"Full marks!\" [2429] \"Kitchener, tell me, do you now understand who the old woman and the young man who could be her son in room 317 were talking about? You were always confused about who Southy and Samuel Kid were talking about in room 317.\" [2430] \"I can't say I understand. Oh, I still don't understand.\" [2431] \"Let's review what Rafael Bockarov said. He heard South Nancy Dunn impersonating Harriet Westbury say, 'He no longer trusts her. He doesn't like her now. It was her choice to go. She's old enough to be his mother now.' Think about the phrase 'He doesn't like her now.' That's the premise, and then comes the two reasons for the 'not liking.' One of them is that she's old enough to be his mother now. No, 'Now, she's old enough to be his mother.' Don't you understand, Carter? If she's old enough to be his mother now, she should have always been old enough to be his mother. There's no other possibility!\" [2432] \"Isn't it just a long sentence?\" I said. \"I mean, the sentence can still make sense without the word 'now.' 'He doesn't like her. It was her choice to go. She's old enough to be his mother.'\" [2433] \"But, my friend, that would be absurd!\" Poirot exclaimed. \"It's illogical. The sentence has the word 'now,' and we can't pretend it's not there, ignore the word 'now,' it's right there in our ears!\" [2434] \"I'm afraid I can't agree with your point of view,\" I said uneasily. \"If I must try to think about it, I would say that the sentence is trying to convey that before she left, the young man didn't particularly care or notice the age difference between them, perhaps not so obvious. Now that she's no longer youthful, he has moved on and fallen in love with a younger, more attractive woman and trusts her\u2014\"\n[2435] Poirot couldn't help but interrupt my words, his face turning red with impatience. \"In my opinion, your guess is absurd, Carter! Listen to me! Listen to the original sentence again, the order of the words is like this, 'He doesn't like her now, it was her choice to go, she's old enough to be his mother now!' First, he doesn't like her, then comes the second reason! The sentence structure is clear, both of these unfortunate reasons are 'now' situations, not the past.\" [2436] \"You don't have to yell at me, Poirot. I understand your point, but I still can't agree. Not everyone is as concerned with word choice as you are. My interpretation must be correct, yours is wrong, just as you said, there's no other possibility. You just said it yourself, if she's old enough to be his mother now, then she should have always been old enough to be his mother.\" [2437] \"Ketsepalu, Ketsepalu, I'm starting to lose hope in you! Think about what comes after this sentence. Raffles Burbank heard Samuel Kid, pretending to be Richard Nix, say, 'I don't agree with what you just said about being old enough to be his mother, I don't agree at all.' In response, Nancy Drew, pretending to be Harriet, said, 'Well, neither of us can prove we're right, so let's agree to disagree.' Why can't they prove themselves right? Is a woman old enough to be a man's mother not a simple biological fact? If she's only four years older than him, of course, she's not old. Is that not obvious? If she's twenty years older, then she's old enough to be his mother. That's also indisputable.\" [2438] \"What if she's thirteen years older?\" Jean Hobbs closed her eyes and said, \"Or twelve? There are always exceptions... Of course, I'm not talking about this particular case.\" [2439] It seems that Jean understands Poirot's point, but I don't. [2440] \"Thirteen years, twelve years, it doesn't matter!\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 3:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[2248] \"You also told me that Ada or Harriet said something else, but you can't remember. Then the person you thought was Richard Nigg said, 'Does he have a brain? I don't think he does. I don't agree with what you just said about 'old enough to be his mother.' At that point, the woman called Harriet smiled and said, 'Well, we can't prove who's right, can we? Let's agree to disagree.' Is that right?\" [2249] Raphael Burbank confirmed Poirot's recollection was accurate. [2250] \"Good. Mr. Burbank, I believe that the line you can't remember was said by Harriet, and I'm one hundred percent sure that in that room, you didn't hear Ada Gransbury say a single word! And you didn't see her face because she was sitting with her back to the door.\" [2251] Baxter furrowed his brow, concentrated for a moment, and then said, \"I think you're right, Mr. Poirot. Yes, I didn't see Miss Edith Gransbury's face. And... after your reminder, I remember that I didn't hear her speak.\" [2252] \"Of course you didn't hear her speak, sir. The reason is simple: the Edith Gransbury you saw at 7:15, sitting with her back to the door and slumped in the chair, was already dead. There was a corpse in Room 317 when you brought the afternoon tea!\" [2253] Chapter 24\n[2254] The Blue Pot and Bowl\n[2255] There were a few screams of terror in the dining room, and I almost screamed myself. It was too strange! Thanks to my work at Scotland Yard, I've seen many corpses, but none were as terrifying as the sight of a dead woman sitting upright, as if she were enjoying tea with her friends. [2256] Poor Raphael Burbak trembled all over and his lips quivered uncontrollably. He had been so close to the monster that no sane person would have done the same. [2257] \"That's why the afternoon tea had to be delivered to Miss Ada Gransbury's room,\" Poirot continued. \"Room 238, where Mr. Richard Nigges lived, should have been the most convenient meeting place for the three victims, as it was between the other two rooms. And, without Mr. Nigges having to say anything, the afternoon tea would naturally be charged to his account. But they couldn't let Raphael Burbak see the three victims alive at seven fifteen in Room 238! If they had, they would have had to remove Miss Ada Gransbury's corpse from her room, carry it through the hotel corridor, and transport it to the lower-level Room 238. That would have been too risky and someone would have seen it.\" [2258] The faces of the bewildered people were now filled with shock. I imagine that Luca Lazzari might soon need to hire new staff. I certainly don't want to come back to the Brawnsham Hotel after the case is over. I imagine that most of the people in this room must feel the same way. [2259] Poirot continued to explain: \"Ladies and gentlemen, please carefully recall Mr. Richard Nigges' generosity. Oh, he was so generous, insisting on paying for the tea and paying for a car to pick up Harriet and Ada from the station. Why didn't they come together on the train and then share a car to the hotel? Since Mr. Richard Nigges knew that he, Harriet, and Ada were all about to die, why did he care so much about whether the food and drink bills were charged to his name?\" [2260] This was a good question. Each point Poirot made was on target and something I should have thought of. Regardless, I hadn't noticed how many inconsistencies there were in Miss Hobbs' statement. How could I have overlooked so many obvious gaps? [2261] Poirot said, \"The person pretending to be Richard Nigges was only trying to make Rafael Borker see him at 7:15 and 7:30 to see Thomas Brine. He didn't care about the bills! He knew that he and his accomplices wouldn't have to pay and that he had taken the food outside. So, how did he take the food out? He put it in a suitcase! Canterbury, do you remember seeing a homeless man near the hotel when we took the bus? The homeless man was eating from a suitcase, do you remember? You said he was 'a homeless man with cream.' Tell me, did you see him eating cream clearly?\" [2262] \"Oh, my goodness! Yes, I saw! He was eating a piece... of cake with cream inside.\" [2263] Poirot nodded and said, \"He found a discarded suitcase near Brookes Han hotel that contained tea for three people! Now, my friend, let me test your memory again. Do you remember, when I first came to Brookes Han, you told me that Miss Edith Gransberry brought a lot of clothes, enough to fill the closet? But her room only had one suitcase, the same size as Richard Nigges's and Harriet Sipper's, but they brought fewer clothes. When I asked you to put Miss Gransberry's clothes in her suitcase this afternoon, what did you find?\" [2264] \"It didn't fit. \" I felt like a big fool again. I felt foolish earlier because I couldn't even organize Miss Edith Gransberry's luggage, and now there's another reason. [2265] \"You were blaming yourself,\" Poirot said. \"You always like to criticize yourself, but in reality, it's impossible to fit all the clothes in, because they were brought in two suitcases. Even Hercule Poirot couldn't fit them into one suitcase.\" [2266] He turned around and said to the people in the restaurant, \"It was while throwing away the suitcase full of food and returning to the hotel that the man met Thomas Brinell, the receptionist of Blauxham, who is the door of this restaurant. Why did he deliberately discuss the bill with Brinell? There is only one reason: to deepen Brinell's impression and make him remember that Richard Nigges was still alive at half past seven. But the man who impersonated Mr. Nigges made a mistake. He said, 'Nigges can afford it, while Harriet Sipper and Ada Gransbury can't.' But that's not true! Henry Nigges, Richard's brother, can prove that Richard has no income and has little left in savings. But the man playing Richard Nigges didn't know that. He thought Richard was a gentleman, a former lawyer, and must have a lot of money.\" [2267] \"Henry Nigges first told me and Ketaki that since moving to Devonshire, his brother Richard has become gloomy and preoccupied, like a recluse with no desire for life. Mr. Henry Nigges, am I right?\" [2268] \"Yes, that's right.\" [2269] \"A recluse! Would a recluse like sherry and cake, would he chat with two women in a luxurious hotel in London? No! The man who took the afternoon tea from Rafael Bocaccio and the sherry from Thomas Brinell was not Richard Nigges. That man complimented Mr. Brinell on his efficiency and said something like this: 'You're efficient, and I believe you can get the job done - charge the cost of food and drink to my account, Richard Nigges in room 238 at Blauxham.' He chose his words carefully to make Thomas Brinell believe this Richard Nigges, and they must have met before. Mr. Brinell may have felt guilty at the time because he couldn't remember meeting this Mr. Nigges before, so he decided to remember him this time. He wanted to remember that he had met this man at least twice. Of course, working in a big hotel in London, he has to meet hundreds of people every day! I'm sure there are often cases where he remembers their names and appearances, but they forget what they look like - for him, they're just 'guests'!\" [2270] \"Excuse me, Mr. Poirot, forgive me,\" Luca Lazzari rushed up and said, \"generally speaking, you are right, but there are exceptions. Thomas Brinell is an exception, he has a super memory and can remember guests' faces and names very well. He has a super memory!\" [2271] Poirot smiled approvingly and said, \"Is that so? Well, it seems I'm right.\" [2272] \"About what?\" I asked. [2273] \"Please be patient, Ketton-Cremer, I'll explain one by one. The day before the murder, on Wednesday, when Richard Nix checked into the hotel, the man impersonating him was in the lobby. He might have been casing the place for his upcoming impersonation. Anyway, he saw Richard Nix. How did he recognize Richard Nix? I'll get to that in a moment. He saw Thomas Breen check him in and hand him the room key. The next evening, after the man impersonating Nix took the afternoon tea and disposed of the food outside, on his way back to Room 317, he met Thomas Breen. He was a quick-witted man who immediately saw an excellent opportunity to further mislead the police. He approached Breen, greeted him, and claimed to be Richard Nix, reminding Breen that they had met before. [2274] \"In fact, Thomas Breen had never met this man before, but he remembered the name Richard Nix because he had given him the room key. When suddenly someone spoke to him in a confident, friendly, and intimate manner, claiming to be Richard Nix, Thomas Breen naturally believed he was Richard Nix. Although he couldn't remember the face, he would only blame himself for being careless.\" [2275] Thomas Breen's face turned as red as wine. [2276] Poirot continued, \"The imposter who pretended to be Richard Nigges ordered another sherry. Why? To prolong his meeting with Thomas Brineau and make him remember him? Or to calm his nerves with alcohol? Both are possible.\" [2277] \"Now, allow me to change the subject. We found cyanide in the remaining sherry, just like in the cups of Miss Harriet Sipper and Miss Ada Gransbury. However, it was not the tea or sherry that killed these three victims. It couldn't have been. The murders had already occurred when the drinks were delivered to the rooms. The poisoned sherry glass and two tea cups were placed on the small table next to the body to create a false crime scene, leading us to believe the incident occurred after 7:15 p.m. In fact, Miss Harriet Sipper, Miss Ada Gransbury, and Mr. Richard Nigges were poisoned earlier using a different method. There is a glass cup by the sink in each room, isn't there, Mr. Lazari?\" [2278] \"Yes, Mr. Poirot, there is one in each room.\" [2279] \"So, I think they must have ingested the poison \u2013 in water. Afterward, the killer cleaned the glass that held the poison and placed it back by the sink. Mr. Brineau! Poirot suddenly called out, causing the reception assistant to shrink his neck, as if someone had shot him, \"You don't like talking in public, but when we first gathered in this restaurant, you mustered the courage to tell us that you had encountered Mr. Nigges in the corridor. But you didn't mention the sherry, even though I specifically asked about it. Later, you came to me and added the sherry incident. I asked you why you didn't mention it before, and you didn't answer. I didn't understand the reason at the time, but my friend, Caderousse, said some very insightful words that inspired me. He said you are a responsible person, and now involved in a murder case, you are concealing something that makes you very embarrassed and you are sure it has nothing to do with the case. He was right on the mark, wasn't he?\" [2280] Mr. Brineau nodded slightly. [2281] \"Allow me to explain,\" Poirot raised his voice, even though it was already quite loud, \"When we gathered in this restaurant last time, I asked everyone if they had delivered sherry to Mr. Nigus' room. No one spoke. Why didn't Thomas Brine say, 'I didn't deliver it to his room, but I did bring him a sherry'? Poirot will tell you! He didn't because he had doubts and didn't want to risk saying something uncertain. [2282] \"Among all the hotel staff, only Mr. Brine has seen one of the three victims \u2013 or rather, he was misled to believe he had seen Richard Nigus more than once. He knows he gave a man who claimed to be Richard Nigus a sherry, and the man acted as if he had seen him before, but he didn't look like the Richard Nigus he remembered. Don't forget, Mr. Lazari told us that Mr. Brine has excellent memory for names and faces. That's why he didn't speak when I asked about the sherry! He was thinking it through. A voice whispered in his head: 'It must be him, the same person. But it's not him, I can recognize him.'\" [2283] \"Thank God,\" said Thomas Brine. \"Poirot, every word you say is true.\" [2284] \"Let me explain further,\" Poirot raised his voice again, \"When we gathered in this restaurant last time, I asked everyone if they had delivered sherry to Mr. Nigus' room. No one spoke. Why didn't Thomas Brine say, 'I didn't deliver it to his room, but I did bring him a sherry'? Poirot will tell you! He didn't because he had doubts and didn't want to risk saying something uncertain. [2285] \"Among all the hotel staff, only Mr. Brine has seen one of the three victims \u2013 or rather, he was misled to believe he had seen Richard Nigus more than once. He knows he gave a man who claimed to be Richard Nigus a sherry, and the man acted as if he had seen him before, but he didn't look like the Richard Nigus he remembered. Don't forget, Mr. Lazari told us that Mr. Brine has excellent memory for names and faces. That's why he didn't speak when I asked about the sherry\n[2286] \"Of course,\" Poirot said without modesty. \"Please remember, ladies and gentlemen, the same name does not necessarily mean the same person. When Mr. Lazarus described the registration of Miss Jenny Hobbs to me, I also thought she was the same Miss Jenny Hobbs I had met at the 'Caf\u00e9 Joyeux.' It sounded similar, light hair, dark brown hat, light brown coat. But two men had only seen a woman fitting that description once, and they could not be sure they saw the same person. [2287] \"This point troubled me. I was already suspicious that the corpse of Richard Nigel I had seen at the crime scene and the living Richard Nigel that Mr. Raphael Barker and Mr. Thomas Brineau had seen on the night of the crime were two different men. Then I remembered that Mr. Thomas Brineau had checked in Mr. Richard Nigel on the Wednesday when he arrived at the Brawnsham Hotel. If I'm right, then this Richard Nigel is the real Richard Nigel. Suddenly, I understood Mr. Thomas Brineau's dilemma. How could he say in front of everyone that a man seemed to have two faces? They would think he was crazy! [2288] \"You're half crazy now, Mr. Poirot,\" Samuel Kidder sneered. [2289] Poirot ignored him and continued, \"The imposter may not look very similar to Richard, but I am certain he can imitate his voice very well. He is very good at imitation - Mr. Kidder, isn't he? [2290] \"Don't believe this man! He's a fraud!\" [2291] \"No, Mr. Kidder, you're the fraud. You've impersonated me more than once.\" [2292] At this point, Phyllis Springle, sitting in the back of the restaurant, stood up and said, \"You should all believe Mr. Poirot. He's telling the truth, and it's all fact. I've heard Samuel Kidder mimic his accent before. With my eyes closed, I couldn't tell the difference.\" [2293] \"Samuel Kidder doesn't just deceive people with his voice,\" Poirot said. \"When I first met him, he pretended to be a sloppy, intellectually challenged man. His shirt was stained, and he had lost a button. His beard was half-shaved. Mr. Kidder, tell these people why you went to such lengths to make yourself look so unkempt?\" [2294] Samuel Kidder stared resolutely ahead, saying nothing, his eyes filled with disgust. [2295] \"Well, if you don't want to say, I'll explain for you. Mr. Kidder escaped from the window of Richard Nigus's room 238 and climbed down. His face was scratched by the branches outside. A well-dressed, well-groomed man with a scratch on his face would be conspicuous and arouse suspicion, wouldn't he? A well-dressed person wouldn't leave a scar from a razor. Mr. Kidder didn't want me to think along these lines, didn't want me to guess that he had recently climbed out of a window and down a tree, so he created an unkempt appearance. He pretended he couldn't shave himself properly, that he would hurt himself if he tried, to avoid causing more scratches. Poirot would think it was only natural for such a sloppy man to cut himself with a razor. That's what I initially thought. \" [2296] \"Wait, Poirot,\" I said. \"If you say that Samuel Kid climbed out of Richard Nigges' window...\"\n[2297] \"Does it mean he killed Mr. Nigges? No, he didn't. He helped the murderer kill Richard Nigges. As for the murderer... I haven't gotten to that yet.\" [2298] \"Yes, you haven't gotten there yet,\" I said bluntly. \"You also didn't say who the three people were in Room 317 when Rafael Bacard sent the afternoon tea. You only said that the three victims were already dead at that time.\" [2299] \"Yes, at 7:15, the three people in Room 317 included the deceased Edith Gransbury. But she was placed on a chair, and if you didn't see her face, you would think she was still alive. The other one was Samuel Kid, who impersonated Richard Nigges.\" [2300] \"Yes, I guessed that, but who was the third person? The one who impersonated Harriet Sipper and talked about gossip? It couldn't have been Jane Hobbs. As you said, Jane was on her way to 'The Happy Coffee Shop' at that time.\" [2301] \"Ah, yes, the woman who loved to gossip,\" Poirot said. \"I'll tell you who she is, my friend. It was Nancy Duane.\" [2302] There was a chorus of shocked exclamations in the restaurant. [2303] \"Oh, no, Poirot sir,\" Luca Lazzari said.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nIf someone commits a crime, they must face some form of legal sanction, without personal bias or a desire for revenge. The desire for revenge taints everything, and that's true evil.\" [1674] \"The desire for revenge.\" Nancy Dunn shuddered as she repeated the words, \"That's it. Harriet Sipper was filled with a desire for revenge, and it was disgusting.\" [1675] \"But you didn't condemn Harriet Sipper in anger in the story,\" I said, \"You only said her actions were disgusting and made you very sad. But it seems you're angrier at Jennie Hobbs than her.\" [1676] \"I think so,\" sighed Nancy. \"I used to like Harriet. When my husband William and I first moved to Glebeholme, Harriet and George Sippel were our best friends. After George's death, Harriet turned into a monster. It's hard to condemn someone you once liked very much, didn't you notice?\" [1677] \"I couldn't,\" said Poirot. [1678] \"I can't. I would see their bad behavior as a manifestation of a pathology, not the real them. But I cannot forgive Harriet for what she did to Patrick, and I cannot convince myself to forgive her. And I think the one who suffers the most from this transformation is herself.\" [1679] \"Do you consider her a victim?\" [1680] \"Yes, she's young and has lost her beloved husband, and she's very unlucky! I believe one can be both a victim and a perpetrator.\" [1681] \"You and Harriet have something in common,\" Poirot said. \"You both lost your husbands at a young age.\" [1682] \"It sounds callous, but we can't compare ourselves,\" Nancy said. \"George Sipper was Harriet's whole life. I married William because he was intelligent, gave me security, and I needed to get away from home, away from my father.\" [1683] \"Oh, yes. Alcun Johnson.\" Poirot said. \"I remembered who he was after leaving your house last time. At the end of the last century, your father was a notorious Anglo-Russian radical and spent some time in prison.\" [1684] \"He's a dangerous man.\" Nancy said. \"I don't want to talk about his... views, but I must say. He believes that anyone who stands in the way of making the world better should be killed, however many it takes, and what makes the world 'better' is entirely up to him! Oh, for heaven's sake, can a world be made better by bloodshed and slaughter? That group only knows destruction and devastation, and they speak of hope and dreams with hatred and anger. How can they make the world better?\" [1685] \"I completely agree with your views, madam. A movement fueled by anger and hatred will not make our lives better. It's impossible because the source is wrong!\" [1686] I almost agreed, but I didn't say it. No one would be interested in my thoughts. [1687] \"When I met William Duane,\" said Nancy, \"I didn't fall in love with him, I just liked him and respected him. He was calm, humble, and spoke or acted with decorum. For example, if he didn't return a book to the library by the due date, he would be very upset.\" [1688] \"A man with a conscience.\" [1689] \"Yes, he was humble and measured. If something stood in his way, he first thought of going around it, rather than moving it. I knew he wouldn't get along with my father, who was determined to make the world uglier through violence. William liked art and beautiful things, and in that regard, he was like me.\" [1690] \"I understand, madam. But you don't love William Duane as crazily as Harriet Seppel loved her husband, do you?\" [1691] \"Yes, the one I am crazy about is Patrick Eve. From the moment I saw him, my heart belonged to him alone. I would be willing to give up my life for him. It was only when I lost him that I understood the pain Harriet felt when she lost George. People think they can imagine that pain, but they can't. I remember, after George's funeral, Harriet begged me to pray to God to let her die so she could reunite with him sooner. I refused her request and told her that time could heal the pain, and one day she would find new life force.\" [1692] Nancy paused to calm herself down and continued, \"Unfortunately, she really did find it. She found joy in tormenting others. The woman who was killed recently at the Blaxland Hotel in London was the widow Harry, and the Harry I knew and liked had already gone with her husband George.\" [1693] She suddenly looked at me, \"You found me angry with Jenny, but I don't have the right to be angry with her. We both betrayed Patrick, and in that sense, we are both guilty.\" [1694] Nancy covered her face with her hands and began to cry. \"Here, here, ma'am,\" Poirot handed her a handkerchief, \"How did you betray Patrick Eve? You said you would be willing to give your life for him.\" [1695] \"I am as bad as Jenny and a despicable coward! I admitted to the villagers that I loved Patrick and had secret meetings with him, but I didn't tell the truth. Oh, the secret meetings were true, and Patrick and I fell madly in love, but... \" Nancy couldn't finish her sentence. She buried her head in the handkerchief and cried uncontrollably, shaking her shoulders. \"I think I understand, ma'am. At the King's Head Inn, you told the villagers that your relationship with Patrick Eve was still pure, but that was a lie. Did Poirot guess correctly?\" [1696] Nancy gave a despairing cry. \"I couldn't bear those rumors,\" she sobbed. \"What about communicating with the dead to make money? Those stories were too terrible, even children would hiss at him on the street\u2026 I was terrified! You can't imagine how terrible it is to have so many accusations and condemnations, and against one person, a good man!\" [1697] I could imagine. I could vividly imagine the scene, wishing she wouldn't go on. [1698] \"I had to do something, Mr. Poirot. So I thought, I would use pure and beautiful things to fight against lies! That's the truth. I loved Patrick, and Patrick loved my truth. But I was also afraid, so I stained our truth with lies! That was my fault. I was so flustered that I couldn't think rationally. My cowardice and lies stained our beautiful love. Our relationship was not pure, and I lied. I felt I had no choice. I was so cowardly, so shameful!\" [1699] \"You're too hard on yourself, madam.\" [1700] Nancy wiped her eyes and continued, \"I wish you were right. Why didn't I tell all the truth? I should have stood up for Patrick and refuted those terrible slanders. It would have been a noble thing to do, but I ruined everything. That's why I curse myself every day.\" [1701] \"Why didn't you tell the truth?\" asked Poirot. [1702] \"I think I wanted to spare Francis some pain, to avoid a bigger scandal. But then Patrick and Francis both committed suicide, and all hope of making things right was gone.\u201d Nancy thought for a moment and added, \"I know they committed suicide. No matter what others say.\" [1703] \"Is there any controversy about this?\" asked Poirot. [1704] \"The doctors and all the official records say they died from accidents, but no one in Grange Hall believes it. Christianity considers suicide a sin. I think the village doctor said it was an accident to protect Patrick and Francis's reputation. He liked them very much and stood up for them alone. Dr. Flavard, he's a good man, one of the few good people in Grange Hall. He knew it was evil slander from the start.\" [1705] \"Or truth in return for truth?\" said Poirot. [1706] \"Oh, yes, indeed.\" Nancy looked surprised, \"Oh, dear, I've ruined your handkerchief.\" [1707] \"It's okay, I have another one. Madame, I have one more question for you. Do you know Samuel Kid?\" [1708] \"No, should I?\" [1709] \"Did he live in Grailhame when you lived there?\" [1710] \"No, he didn't. Whoever he is, he's lucky.\" Nancy said sadly. [1711] Chapter 17\n[1712] Old Wife Young Husband\n[1713] The guests left, and it was just me and Poirot in the room. \"So, Nancy Drew and Margaret Earnshaw both believe the Ives were suicides, but the official records say it was an accident. Ambrose Flavard lied about it being an accident to protect Patrick and Frances Ives' reputation from further damage.\" [1714] \"It's strange,\" I said, \"Margaret didn't mention this.\" [1715] \"I suspect we may have found the reason why you were sworn not to speak to the doctor about it. What if Ambrose Flavard was so proud of his lie\u2014so proud that if asked, he would want to confess. And if Margaret Earnshaw wanted to protect him\u2026\"\n[1716] \"Yes,\" I agreed. \"That's probably why she wanted me to stay away from him.\" [1717] \"To protect\u2014I understand that kind of psychology too well,\" Poirot's voice was very excited. [1718] \"Don't blame yourself for Jenny anymore, Poirot. You couldn't have protected her.\" [1719] \"You're right, Carrington, no one could protect Jenny, not even Hercule Poirot. I only realized it too late\u2014it was too late. But there's one thing that's interesting, isn't it? This time there's blood, and before there was only poison\u2014no blood.\" [1720] \"What I've always wondered is where Jenny's body is. The entire Bluxton Hotel has been searched from top to bottom, and nothing has been found!\" [1721] \"Don't ask yourself where it is, Katchadourian, where it is doesn't matter. Ask yourself why. Why did you take the body away with a laundry cart, a suitcase, or a handcart? Why did you move it? Why didn't you leave it in the hotel room like the other three bodies?\" [1722] \"Oh? What's the answer? You know the answer, tell me quickly.\" [1723] \"Yes,\" said Poirot, \"these can all be explained, but I'm worried it's an unpleasant explanation.\" [1724] \"I want to hear it, whether it's happy or not.\" [1725] \"I'll tell you another thing, Brooks Han Hotel's employees have only seen Harriet Sipper, Ada Gransbury, or Richard Niggles once, except for one person, Thomas Brineau. He saw Richard Niggles twice: once on Wednesday evening, when Niggles arrived at the hotel and Brineau met him; and the second time on Thursday evening, when they met in the hallway, and Niggles asked him for a glass of sherry.\" Poirot smiled contentedly after saying this, \"Think about it, Katchadourian, do you see anything from it?\" [1726] \"Nothing.\" [1727] \"Ah!\" [1728] \"Leave me alone, Poirot! I've never heard anyone say 'ah' with such anger.\" [1729] \"I've already told you, my friend, not to expect others to give you the answers.\" [1730] \"I'm really stumped! From any angle, Nancy Duane is clearly the killer, but she has a Mrs. Wallace's alibi. So who else could want to kill Harriet Spear, Ada Grimsby, Richard Nix, and Jenny Hobbs?\" I paced around the living room, frustrated at not seeing any clues, \"On the other hand - although I still think your suspicions are crazy - if the killer is Henry Nix or Raphael Buccar or Thomas Brenell, what would their motive be? Who among them has any connection to the tragedy that occurred sixteen years ago in Gleeholm?\" [1731] \"Henry Nigges has the most mundane and commonplace motive for murder: money. He told us that his brother was squandering his fortune, right? He also told us that his wife did not approve of Richard being kicked out of the house. If Richard Nigges were dead, he would not have to support him. If Richard had not died, he might have spent a large sum of money from his brother.\" [1732] \"But why kill Harriet Spear and Edith Gransbury? And Jenny Hobbs, why would Henry Nigges kill her?\" [1733] \"I don't know, but I have some suspicions.\" [1734] \"I think we need to investigate further.\" [1735] \"Should we add Margaret Ernst and Ambrose Flavard to the list of suspects? They didn't love Patrick Yves, but they could also have wanted to kill for revenge. According to Margaret Ernst herself, she was alone at home on the night of the incident. We don't know where Dr. Flavard was, as you promised not to find him, and\u2014ah! You did keep your promise. Poirot will have to go to Grelling himself.\" [1736] \"I said you should have come with me.\" I reminded him, \"But if you went, you wouldn't have had the chance to talk to Nancy Duane, Rafael Burbank, and others. Oh, by the way, Burbank heard Harry, Ada, and Richard Nix talking about an old woman and a young man \u2014 assuming we believe what he said \u2014 I've been thinking about who they could be, and I've made a list of all the couples I can think of.\u201d I took the list out of my pocket. (I admit, I was hoping to please Poirot, but he didn't react, or maybe he was pretending not to.) [1737] \"George and Harriet Sipper,\" I read out loud, \"Patrick and Frances Ife, Patrick Ife and Nancy Duane, William Duane and Nancy Duane, Charles and Margaret Ernst, Richard Nix and Ada Gransberry. But none of these couples have a woman who is older than the man, and of course, they wouldn't be described as 'old enough to be his mother'.\u201d\n[1738] \"Ah, Poirot said impatiently, \"Don't think about it, my friend. How do you know that the couple even exists?\u201d\n[1739] I stared at him, wondering if he had lost his mind. \"Well, Walter Stuker said so at the King's Head Inn, and Rafael Burbank heard \u2014\"\n[1740] \"No, no,\" Poirot rudely interrupted me, \"you didn't notice the details. At the King's Head Inn, Walter Stuker said that the woman ended the relationship with the man, right? And Rafael Burbank overheard the three victims saying that the man no longer loved the woman, while the woman still craved the man's love. How could they be the same couple? On the contrary, they couldn't possibly be the same!\u201d\n[1741] \"You're right,\" I said,