t I find myself more reliable. Before you tell me your story, you asked me if I had retired, didn't you? You went to great lengths to make sure I had no authority to arrest anyone and that I was not part of the country's law enforcement. I must assure you that I am not, but I told you I had a friend at Scotland Yard. You confided in me not because I had no authority to arrest the killer, but because you knew I had some influence in the police. You wanted to see Nancy Deane framed and convicted of murder and hanged!\" [1822] \"That is not what I had in mind!\" Jane turned her tear-stained face to me, saying, \"Please, don't let him go on!\" [1823] \"I will stop soon,\" Poirot said. \"Madam, the waitress at 'The Happy Caf\u00e9' said you are a regular customer. The waitresses like to gossip about their customers. I suppose you overheard them talking about me: a meticulous European gentleman with a mustache, who was once a police officer in Europe, has a friend named Cacoghull at Scotland Yard, and appears at 'The Happy Caf\u00e9' every Thursday at 7:30 p.m. for dinner. Yes, madam, you knew how to find me, and you knew that Hercule Poirot would be the ideal choice to help you achieve your evil plans! So you came to the caf\u00e9 with a terrified look, but it was a ruse, a performance! You stared out the window for a long time, as if you were afraid someone was following you, but in reality, you could see nothing but your reflection in the window. A waitress noticed you staring at her instead of the street outside. You were calculating, weren't you? 'Will someone suspect that my terrified look was fake? Will the observant waitress see through my act and interfere with my plans?'\" [1824] I stood up, saying, \"Poirot, I don't doubt what you're saying, but you can't keep haranguing a poor woman without giving her a chance to defend herself.\" [1825] \"Be quiet, Carrington. Have I not made myself clear? Mrs. Hobbs is very skilled at putting on an act of helplessness, hiding her cunning and calculating nature beneath her exterior. [1826] \"You are heartless!\" cried Jane, sobbing. [1827] \"On the contrary, madam. Fear not, the time will come when you will speak, but for now, I have one more question for you. You told me, 'Please do not let them speak!' How did you know that Nancy Durand would put a cufflink in their mouths after killing them? I am curious as to how you knew. Did Mrs. Durand threaten to do so? I can imagine the killer threatening violence, saying things like, 'If I catch you, I'll cut your throat,' but I cannot imagine a killer saying, 'After I kill you, I'll put a cufflink with initials engraved on it in your mouth.' Despite my vivid imagination, I cannot imagine anyone saying such a thing!\" [1828] \"I apologize, madam, but there is one more thing. Whatever wrong you did in the tragedy involving Patrick and Frances Ives, the three people who should bear the same or at least equal blame are Harriet Sipper, Ada Gransbury, and Richard Nigg. They believed your lies and accused the Ives of their own accord. Moreover, at the 'Joyful Coffee House,' you told me, 'Only when I am dead will justice be served.' You emphasized 'I' and said 'only when I am dead.' This made me think that you already knew that Harriet Sipper, Ada Gransbury, and Richard Nigg had been killed. But now, with the evidence at hand, I believe that these three murders had not yet occurred at that time.\" [1829] \"Please, stop! Stop!\" cried Jane, sobbing. [1830] \"Please wait a moment longer. When you told me, 'Only when I am dead will justice be served,' it was approximately 7:45, but now we know that the employees at the Browns Hotel discovered the three bodies at 8:10. So, Jane Hobbs, how did you know about these murders beforehand?\" [1831] \"If you can stop, I will tell you everything! I've been keeping secrets and lying, which has been a torture for me. I can't take it anymore!\" [1832] \"Okay.\" Poirot said calmly, his tone suddenly friendly, \"Does this scare you? Have you realized that you can't fool Poirot?\" [1833] \"I understand. Let me tell you the story from beginning to end. Finally, I can say it, finally I can breathe a sigh of relief.\" [1834] Jenny began to tell the story. Hercule Poirot and I did not interrupt her until she finished. Here is her story, which I hope to faithfully and completely convey. [1835] * * *\n[1836] \"Sam Kidd\" and \"a kid\" sound very similar. [1837] Chapter Nineteen\n[1838] The Truth Revealed\n[1839] I ruined the life of the only man I ever loved, and destroyed my own. [1840] I didn't mean for things to turn out this way. I never imagined that the cruel words I uttered would cause such devastation. I should have thought twice, held my tongue, but I was hurt and vulnerable, and my anger got the better of me. [1841] I loved Patrick Ivie wholeheartedly. I tried not to, but as soon as I started serving him, making his bed at Jesus College, Cambridge, where he was studying, I was engaged to Sam Kidd. I liked Sam a lot, but after meeting Patrick, my heart belonged to him, and I knew that no matter how hard I tried, this feeling would not change. Patrick had everything a man should have. He also liked me, but to him, I was just a servant. Even after I started speaking like the daughter of a Cambridge teacher, like Frances Ivie, in Patrick's eyes, I was still just a loyal servant, nothing more. [1842] Of course, I knew about his affair with Nancy Dunn. I overheard their conversations, but not intentionally. Knowing how much he loved her made it unbearable for me. I had already accepted that he belonged to Frances, not me, but discovering that he had fallen in love with another woman, one who was neither his wife nor me, was unbearable. [1843] For a moment, I did consider punishing him, but the thought quickly faded. I wanted to make him as miserable as he had made me. So, I concocted a vile lie and told it to \u2013 oh, my God, told it to Harriet Spear. The lie I fabricated soothed my heart: the sweet nothings Patrick had said to Nancy, words I had overheard more than once, were actually spoken by the late William Dunn, and Patrick was just the messenger. I know it sounds absurd, but when I told Harriet Spear, for a few seconds, I believed it was true. [1844] Then, Harriet got to work. She spread the rumor throughout the village that Patrick was terrible and unforgivable, and Alice and Richard helped her fan the flames.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nOur attention was completely drawn to the room itself, which was so lavish that I couldn't take my eyes off it. I stood at the door, looking up at the high ceiling, which was decorated with intricate patterns and designs. It was hard to imagine people sitting under such a masterpiece, eating bread and jam, perhaps not even glancing up as they peeled boiled eggs. [422] I was trying to make sense of the complex design of the entire piece, wondering what relationship existed between the different patterns on the ceiling, when Luca Lazari rushed towards me. He spoke to me in a quivering voice, interrupting my appreciation of the artistic masterpiece above me. \"Mr. Kitchpur, Mr. Polo, I sincerely apologize! I was eager to assist you with such an important task, but I misinterpreted the message! I heard too many rumors and wanted to collect them, but I didn't do it properly. It's all my fault! No one else is to blame. Oh\u2026\"\n[423] Lazari stopped, looking back at the hundred or so men and women gathered in the restaurant. He then turned to his left, facing Polo, puffing out his chest and hanging his hands on either side of his hips, making a comical gesture. I think he was trying to shield his entire staff from Polo's distrustful gaze, believing that as long as Polo couldn't see them, he couldn't blame them. [424] \"Mr. Lazari, what mistake did you make?\" Polo asked. [425] \"A major mistake! You're right, it's impossible. But I hope you understand that the employees standing in front of you are all excellent, and they told me the truth. It was me who distorted the facts and misled you, but it wasn't intentional!\" [426] A short, stout, balding man stood up from the table. He appeared to be around fifty years old, resembling a Jew with his sad eyes, which made him look like a short-eared hound. \"It was me, sir,\" he said. [427] The \"excellent\" employees sat quietly around the table, attentively listening to every word. The atmosphere was tense. I quickly scanned their faces, but saw no smiles. [428] \"I previously told you that the three victims requested their dinner to be served in their respective rooms at 7:15 PM \u2013 separately,\" Lazari said. [429] \"Good. That makes sense. Who was the server you mentioned?\" Polo seemed pleased. [430] A short, stout, balding man stood up from the table. He appeared to be around fifty years old, resembling a Jew with his sad eyes, which made him look like a short-eared hound. \"It was me, sir,\" he said. [431] \"What is your name, sir?\" [432] \"Raphael Bork, sir.\" [433] \"Last night at 7:15, did you deliver the tea to Room 317 to Harriet Sipper, Edith Gainsborough, and Richard Nigges?\" Poirot asked him. [434] \"Not dinner, sir,\" Raphael Bork said. \"Tea, Mr. Nigges ordered. Dinner-time tea. He asked me if it was okay, if he had to order 'the main course' - he used that word. He told me that he and his friends didn't have an appetite for dinner, so they would have tea. I told him, sir, that you can order whatever you want, so he ordered three sandwiches with ham, cheese, smoked salmon, and cucumber, as well as a mixed cake set. And scones, sir, with jam and cream.\" [435] \"What did they drink?\" Poirot asked. [436] \"Tea, sir. All three of them wanted tea.\" [437] \"Oh. Did Mr. Nixey not order sherry?\" [438] Rafael Boccoli shook his head. \"No, sir, no sherry. Mr. Nixey did not order sherry, and I have not delivered any sherry to room 317.\" [439] \"Are you sure?\" [440] \"Absolutely, sir.\" [441] Feeling somewhat embarrassed under the scrutiny of so many eyes and realizing I had not asked a single question, I decided to intervene. \"Has anyone here delivered tea to room 121, where Miss Harriet Sipple resides, or sherry to Mr. Richard Nigges' room, either yesterday or the day before, on Wednesday?\" [442] All heads shook. It seemed that only Raphael Burbak had delivered anything to the rooms of the three victims, bringing \"evening tea\" to room 317 at 7:15 p.m. on Thursday, unless someone was lying. [443] I tried to make sense of the situation: The tea cup in Miss Harriet Sipple's room was fine, so it must have been one of the cups Burbak delivered, as only two remained after the incident. However, since no one had delivered sherry, how did the sherry glass end up in Mr. Richard Nigges' room? [444] Could the killer have come to the hotel with poison, a monogrammed cufflink, and a bottle of Harvey's sweet sherry in his pocket? That seemed far-fetched. [445] Burbak seemed equally concerned. \"Let me confirm, none of you delivered a glass of sherry to Mr. Richard Nigges, either to his room or anywhere else in the hotel?\" [446] \"Mr. Lazarus, is the glass in Mr. Nigg's room a Brouksen Hotel glass?\" [447] \"Yes, it is, Poirot. This is indeed strange. I suppose the server who delivered the wine to Mr. Nigg on Thursday or Wednesday didn't come today, but the staff on those days are here.\" [448] \"Indeed, that is strange,\" Poirot agreed. \"Mr. Buccleuch, when you delivered the 'evening tea' to Miss Adah Gransbury's room, what happened?\" [449] \"I put the items on the table and left, sir.\" [450] \"I see,\" Poirot said. \"Thank you for your cooperation.\" [451] \"Were all three of them in the room, Mrs. Westbury, Miss Garnesbury, and Mr. Nigges?\" [452] \"Yes, sir, they were all there.\" [453] \"Could you describe the situation?\" [454] \"Situation, sir?\" [455] Seeing Mr. Buccleuch's bewildered expression, I interjected, \"Who opened the door for you?\" [456] \"Mr. Nix let me in, sir.\" [457] \"Where are the two ladies?\" I asked. [458] \"Oh, they are sitting on the chairs by the fireplace, chatting. I only spoke to Mr. Nix, not to them. Sir, I put the things on the table by the window and left.\" [459] \"Can you recall what the two ladies were talking about?\" Poirot asked. [460] Burbank lowered his head: \"Oh, sir\u2026\"\n[461] \"This is important, sir. Every detail you can tell us about these three people is important.\" [462] \"Oh\u2026 they were a bit sneaky, sir. They were laughing, too.\" [463] \"Do you mean they were up to no good? How so?\" [464] \"One of them was. Mr. Nixes seemed quite happy. They were talking about an old woman and a young man. Since it was none of my business, I didn't pay much attention.\" [465] \"Do you remember anything specific they said? Who were they talking about?\" [466] \"Sorry, sir, I can't recall. I got the feeling they were talking about an old woman trying to woo a young man, just some gossip, you know.\" [467] \"Sir,\" said Poirot in the most serious tone, \"If you happen to remember any of their conversation \u2013 any at all \u2013 please tell me immediately.\" [468] \"I will, sir. Let me think... It was something like the young man left the old woman and ran off with another woman. Just some boring gossip, that's all.\" [469] \"So...,\" said Poirot, pacing around the room. As he moved, the heads of the hundred and twenty people followed his steps, turning with him as he turned. It was a curious sight. \"Richard Nigges, Harriet Sippele, and Ada Gransbury, a man and two women, whispering about another man and two women in Room 317!\" [470] \"What does it mean, Poirot?\" I asked. [471] \"Probably nothing, but it's interesting. Gossip, laughter, afternoon tea... It means our three victims were not strangers, but friends, and didn't realize they were about to meet their doom.\" [472] Suddenly, I jumped. A young man with black hair and a pale face, sitting at the table in front of me and Poirot, jumped up as if someone had pushed him from below. If it weren't for the terrified expression on his face, I would have thought he was eager to say something. [473] \"This is our new employee, Thomas Breenell, sir,\" Lazari waved at the young man, introducing him to us. [474] \"They're not just acquaintances, sir,\" Breenell spoke after a moment of silence, his voice barely audible, so that even those sitting behind him might not have heard what he was saying, \"They're good friends, very familiar with each other.\" [475] \"They're certainly good friends!\" Lazari exclaimed to the whole room, \"They even eat together!\" [476] \"Many people have to eat with people they don't like every day,\" Polo said. \"Mr. Brineau, please continue.\" [477] \"Last night, I met Mr. Niggles and he was very concerned about the two ladies. Only good friends would act that way,\" Thomas Brineau whispered. [478] \"Did you meet him?\" I asked, \"When? Where?\" [479] \"At 7:30, sir.\" He pointed to the double doors leading to the dining room, and I noticed his arm trembling. \"I was leaving when I saw him heading towards the elevator. He stopped when he saw me and called me over. I thought he was going back to his room.\" [480] \"What did he say?\" Polo asked. [481] \"He...he asked if the dinner bill was charged to his account, to make sure it wasn't charged to those two ladies. He said he had the money and could pay, but Mrs. Westphal and Miss Gransbury couldn't.\" [482] \"Did he say anything else, sir?\" Poirot asked. [483] \"No,\" Burrnell said, as if he would faint if he spoke another word. [484] \"Thank you, Mr. Burrnell,\" I said in a very gentle tone. \"Your information is very helpful. Thank you, Mr. Burbak, and thank everyone else too.\" [485] \"Carterpillar,\" Poirot murmured. \"Most people in the restaurant haven't spoken yet.\" [486] \"They've been listening and considering the matter, and we should be grateful for their attention.\" [487] \"You seem to trust them a lot, don't you? If we have any objections, should we gather all these people again? Let's ask these people... Mr. Burbank turned to the crowd, \"Ladies and gentlemen, we've all heard that Richard Nigg, Harry Voleur, and Edith Gransberry are friends. At seven-fifteen, their dinner was delivered to Room 317. But at seven-thirty, Mr. Burrnell saw Richard Nigg heading towards the elevator. He must have been going back to his room, right? To his own room, 238, or to Room 317 to visit his friends? And where did he come from? His sandwich and cake were delivered just fifteen minutes ago! Did he leave without eating? Or did he only spend a few minutes eating his own portion and then rushed out? Where did he rush off to? What urgent matter required him to leave Room 317? Was it to ensure that the dinner bill was not charged to Harry Voleur or Edith Gransberry's account? He didn't even wait for twenty or thirty minutes or an hour to do this?\" [488] At this point, a robust, brunette woman with curly hair stood up from the back of the restaurant, her forehead furrowed. \"You've asked so many questions all at once, as if I know, as if we all know. But we don't know anything!\" She spoke to Burbank, but looked around the restaurant at everyone. \"I want to go home, Mr. Lazari,\" she whimpered. \"I want to go check on my children to see if they're safe!\" [489] A young woman sitting next to her grabbed her arm, trying to calm her down. \"Sit down, Theresa,\" she said. \"That man just wants to help us. As long as your children aren't near the Brawnsham Hotel, they won't be harmed.\" [490] These soothing words caused both Lazari and Theresa Studd to groan in pain. [491] \"We won't keep you here for long, ma'am,\" I said, \"I promise that Mr. Lazari will let you go home to see your children as soon as we're done. \" Lazari immediately agreed, and Theresa calmed down and became more relaxed. [492] I turned to Pollock and said, \"Richard Nix left Room 317 not to handle the bill issue. He went somewhere else to handle his own business, met Thomas Breen on his way back, and decided to handle the bill issue. I hope this explanation will help everyone in the restaurant understand that we have questions, but we also have answers. Although not all questions have answers at the moment, having answers is better than having none.\" [493] \"Mr. Breen, do you remember the situation when you met Mr. Nix? Was it like Mr. Kacherpohl said, just a coincidence? He didn't come to find you on purpose, did he? On Tuesday, when he checked into the hotel, was it you who greeted him?\" [494] \"You're right, sir. He didn't come to find me on purpose.\" Breen sat in the chair, looking more comfortable, \"He happened to meet me, probably thinking, 'Oh, it's that guy again,' if you understand what I mean.\" [495]\n[496] \"Ladies and gentlemen, that's exactly what happened,\" Poirot said, raising his voice, \"Last night, after the crime, the killer, or someone who knows the killer and decides to help him commit the crime, left a note at the front desk, saying, 'May they rest in peace, 121, 238, 317.' Did anyone happen to see the person who left the note? It's this white piece of paper. Poirot took out the small white piece of paper from his pocket and held it high in the air, \"It was discovered by Mr. John Good at 8:10 p.m. last night. Did anyone notice that at that time, one or more people approached the front desk and behaved abnormally? Think hard! Someone must have seen something!\" [497] Theresa, who had closed her eyes and leaned on her friend, was silent. The restaurant was filled with whispers and gasps as everyone saw the killer's message, which made the triple murder case more realistic. [498] No one provided any more information. It turned out that if you asked a hundred people, you would only be disappointed. [499] Chapter 6\n[500] The Mystery of the Sherry\n[501] Thirty minutes later, Polo and I sat in the \"secret lounge\" mentioned by Lazare, sipping coffee and warming ourselves by the fire, which burned brightly. The \"secret lounge\" was hidden behind the restaurant, not connected to any public corridors. The walls around the lounge were adorned with portraits, which I tried my best not to look at. I would have appreciated a pleasant landscape painting, even a gloomy one. The eyes in the portraits always made me uncomfortable, no matter which artist had painted them. I always felt like the people in the portraits were mocking me, and I couldn't escape their mocking gaze. [502] Just moments ago, Bolo was still passionately delivering a speech like a program host in the restaurant, but now he seems to be feeling down again. \"Are you worrying about Jenny again?\" I asked him. [503] He admitted, \"I don't want to hear news that there's a cufflink in her mouth with the initials PIJ engraved on it. I'm afraid to hear such news.\" [504] \"Considering that you can't do anything right now, I suggest you think about something else.\" I advised him. [505] \"Hello, reality, Kagipur. Alright, let's think about the tea cup then.\" [506] \"Teacups?\" [507] \"Yes. What do you think?\" [508] I thought for a moment and said, \"I don't have any opinion on teacups.\" [509] Polo made an impatient sound. \"Rafael Bacchus sent three teacups to Ada Gransbury's room. Three people, three teacups, that makes sense. But when they found their bodies, there were only two teacups in the room.\" [510] \"The other one was in Harriet Sippler's room, with her body,\" I said. [511] \"Correct, but that's also the most curious part, isn't it? Did Mrs. Spear carry the teacups and saucers back to her room? Would she carry a cup of tea through the hotel corridors, then take the elevator or climb two flights of stairs, regardless of whether there was poison in the tea? If the teacup was full, the tea could spill; if it was half-full or almost empty, there would be no need to carry it. Generally speaking, people drink tea in the room where it's served, don't they?\" [512] \"Generally speaking, yes. But this murderer is clearly far from ordinary, and he shocks me greatly.\" [513] \"And the victims? Are they ordinary people? Are their behaviors normal? Are you trying to make me believe that Harriet Spear carried the teacups back to her room, sat down in a chair, and was about to drink her tea when the murderer knocked on the door and took the opportunity to put cyanide in her tea? And don't forget Richard Nigges. For some reason, he left Edith Gransberry's room but quickly returned to his own room, holding a glass of sherry. But no one had sent him sherry at the hotel.\" [514] \"I believe\u2026\" I said. [515] But Poirot continued, as if he hadn't heard me. \"Ah, yes, Richard Nigges too. The murderer went to his room to find him, and he was sitting there alone, holding a drink, as if saying to the murderer, 'Please put the poison in my drink.' At that moment, was Edith Gransberry alone in Room 317, patiently waiting for the murderer? Sipping her tea slowly, it would be inconsiderate to finish it before the murderer arrived. How could the murderer put cyanide in it then? Where would he put the cyanide?\" [516] \"Damn it, Poirot, what do you want me to say? I know as much as you do! Listen, I just think that these three victims must have argued.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 7:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nThe plan she described was the same as the one she had told me and Poirot about at Samuel Kid's house, including how things had gone awry later. When she mentioned that they would frame Nancy Duane with the murders, ensuring she would be hanged, Louisa Wallace let out a shriek. \"Framing an innocent woman, putting her to death for three murders she did not commit, is not correcting the mistake! This is evil!\" exclaimed St. John Wallace. [2172] No one contradicted him, at least no one spoke out against him. I noticed that Phyllis Spurling did not seem as shocked as most people, and she appeared to be listening intently. [2173] \"I never intended to frame Nancy,\" said Jenny. \"Never! Whether you believe me or not, it doesn't matter.\" [2174] \"Mr. Niggles,\" said Poirot, \"Henry Niggles, do you think your brother Richard would devise such a plan?\" [2175] Henry Niggles stood up and said, \"I don't think so, Mr. Poirot. The Richard I know would never think of killing anyone, of course, the Richard who moved to Devonshire and lived with me sixteen years ago is not the same as the Richard I know. Oh, they look alike on the outside, but they are completely different on the inside. I'm afraid I never really understood the one who came after, so I can't really comment on whether he might have done such a thing.\" [2176] \"Thank you, Mr. Nigges. And thank you, Miss Hobbs.\" He added coldly, \"Please sit down.\" [2177] He turned to the crowd and said, \"Ladies and gentlemen, you see, if Miss Hobbs' words are true, we don't need to arrest anyone, no one is guilty. Adah Gransberry killed her with the permission of Harriet Sipper, Richard Nigges killed her with the permission of Adah Gransberry, and then, since Jenny Hobbs did not go to the hotel as planned, Richard Nigges committed suicide. He locked the door, hid the key behind a loose tile in the fireplace, opened the window, and ended his life, making the scene look like a murder. The police would think that the killer, Nancy Drew, escaped with the key, using the open window and the tree by the window. But according to Jenny Hobbs, there was no killer, everyone died willingly!\" [2178] Poirot looked around the dining room. \"There is no killer,\" he repeated. \"Nevertheless, there are still two living criminals who should be punished. They are Jenny Hobbs and Samuel Kid, who conspired to frame Nancy Drew.\" [2179] \"I hope you lock them up, Mr. Poirot!\" said Louisa Wallace loudly. [2180] \"I don't have the authority to open or close the prison gates, madam. That's the work of my friend Cader and his colleagues. I'm just responsible for uncovering the hidden and the truth. Mr. Samuel Kid, please stand up.\" [2181] Kid stood up, looking uncomfortable. [2182] \"Your task in this plan was to place a note at the hotel's reception desk, was it not? The note read, 'May they rest in peace, 121, 238, 317'?\" [2183] \"Yes, sir. Just as Miss Jennie said, that's exactly what it was.\" [2184] \"Did Miss Jennie give you the note?\" [2185] \"Yes, she gave it to me earlier that day. That morning.\" [2186] \"When did you put it on the table?\" [2187] \"Shortly after eight o'clock in the evening, just like Jenny said. The sooner after eight, the better. But first, make sure no one is around, no one will see me.\" [2188] \"Who said that?\" Polo asked. [2189] \"Jenny.\" [2190] \"Is it Jenny who told you to put the key in Nancy Duane's pocket?\" [2191] \"Yes.\" Kid spoke with annoyance. \"She just said it, and I don't understand why you're asking me again.\" [2192] \"Alright, let me explain. According to Jenny Hobson's original plan, Jenny was supposed to give all three room keys to Samuel Kid after killing Richard, and Samuel would then find a way to plant the keys in a place that would frame Nancy Drew - which, as it turned out, was in Nancy Drew's coat pocket. However, Jenny Hobson said that she didn't go to the Blaukhan Hotel that night. She didn't have the courage to do so. So, I'd like to ask you, Mr. Kid, how did you get the keys to rooms 121 and 317?\" [2193] \"How did I... how did I get those keys?\" [2194] \"Yes, that's my question. Please answer.\" [2195] \"I... Well, if you must know, I got the keys through my own cleverness. I spoke nicely to an employee at the hotel and asked if he could do me a favor and let me use the master key, and he agreed. I returned the key after I was done, and the whole process was very discreet.\" [2196] I was close to Poirot and could hear him make an annoyed sound. He said, \"Which employee, sir? All the employees are here. Point out the one who gave you the master key.\" [2197] \"I can't remember who it was. It was a man. I can't remember people's faces.\" [2198] \"So, you used the master key to enter three rooms?\" [2199] \"No, I only went to room 238. All the keys should have been there, waiting for Jenny to come and take them, but I only found two. As you said, there's another one hidden behind a loose tile in the fireplace, but Mr. Nigel's body was lying there, and I didn't want to stay there too long, so I didn't look for the third key in the room.\" [2200] \"You're lying,\" Poirot said to him. \"It doesn't matter. You'll find that lying won't help you get out of this predicament. Let's continue. No, don't sit down yet. I have another question for you and Jenny Hobbs. You two planned that, after 7:30 p.m. on the night of the murder, Jenny would come to the 'Joyful Coffee House' and tell me the story she had prepared about how scared she was of being murdered, right?\" [2201] \"Yes.\" Jenny answered, looking at Samuel Kid instead of Poirot. [2202] \"I'm sorry, I still have an important question that I haven't figured out. You said you were too scared to carry out the plan, so you didn't go to the hotel at 6 p.m. But it seems that the plan went smoothly without you, the only difference being that Richard Nigges committed suicide, not by you putting poison in his drink. Lady, am I right?\" [2203] \"Yes, that's right.\" [2204] \"So, the only detail that changed was that Richard Nigges committed suicide, so we can say that the death still occurred according to plan: between 7:15 p.m. and 8:00 p.m., after ordering sandwiches and scones. Right, Ms. Hobbs?\" [2205] \"Yes.\" Jenny said, but her voice didn't sound as confident as before. [2206] \"So, I would like to ask, how could you possibly carry out the plan to kill Richard Nigges? You told us that you planned to meet me at 'The Happy Cafe' after seven-thirty, knowing that I always go there for dinner on Thursdays. But from the Bluxton Hotel to 'The Happy Cafe', it takes at least half an hour, no matter what mode of transportation you use. So, even if you killed Adah Gransbury quickly at seven-fifteen and Richard Nigges quickly killed Adah Gransbury, you still wouldn't have been able to be at the 238 room to kill Richard Nigges and then make it to the cafe by seven-thirty. Do you want us to believe that, in all your careful planning of this action, you didn't consider that it was actually impossible?\" [2207] Jenny's face went pale, and I felt mine do the same, even though I couldn't see it. [2208] Poirot's pointed out flaw was so obvious that I hadn't noticed it. I hadn't even thought of it. [2209] Chapter XXIII\n[2210] The True Adah Gransbury\n[2211] Samuel Kidd chuckled softly and turned to face the others in the restaurant. \"Mr. Poirot, you take pride in your investigative abilities, but it seems your powers of observation are not as sharp as you claim. I can say with confidence that I am more familiar with this plan than you are, as Jane has mentioned it countless times. The original plan was not to start at seven fifteen, but at six o'clock. I don't know where these ideas came from. Asking for food at seven fifteen was not part of the plan.\" [2212] \"Yes,\" Jane said, her sharp mind finding a way out of the trap that seemed to have restored her composure. \"I can only say that I was late to the hotel at six o'clock, which caused the plan to be delayed. Others may have wanted to discuss why I didn't show up. I should have been there by then. The discussion would have taken some time.\" [2213] \"Ah, of course. But a few minutes ago, when I asked you if the deaths were carried out according to the agreed plan, between seven fifteen and eight o'clock, you didn't correct me. And neither of you mentioned that the afternoon tea was not part of the plan.\" [2214] \"I apologize for not correcting your statement earlier,\" Jane said. \"I... I mean, it's so oppressive here.\" [2215] \"So, the three executions were scheduled to begin at six o'clock, then?\" [2216] \"Yes, everything was supposed to be completed by 6:45 so that I could arrive at 'The Happy Coffee Shop' at 7:30.\" [2217] \"Then I have another question for you, madam. Why, in your plan, was Mr. Kid supposed to wait an hour before placing the note on the hotel's front desk? By then, Harriet, Ada, and Richard were already dead, and you had left the hotel. Why not at another time, such as 7:15 or 7:30? Why choose 8:00?\" [2218] Jenny recoiled as if struck on the head. \"Why not 8:00?\" she challenged. \"Waiting a little longer won't hurt. [2219] \"Your question is ridiculous, Mr. Poirot,\" said Samuel Kid. [2220] \"Waiting a little longer won't hurt, madam. I agree. So we must ask, what was the purpose of leaving the note? Why not wait until the next morning, when the hotel staff would naturally discover the three bodies? Jenny, don't look at Samuel Kid, look at me, Hercule Poirot! Answer my question.\" [2221] \"I...I don't know! I think maybe Richard...\"\n[2222] \"No! Not Richard!\" Poirot said to her. \"If you don't want to answer my question, I'll answer it for you. You made Mr. Kid put the note on the front desk just after 8:00 because the murder was planned to be done between 7:15 and 8:00!\" [2223] Poirot turned around and faced the crowd with widened eyes, listening quietly. \"Let's think about the afternoon tea that the three victims ordered and delivered to Room 317, Ada Grantham's room. Let's imagine that the three people who willingly sought death were confused because Jane didn't show up. They didn't know what to do, so they went to Ada Grantham's room to discuss it. Mr. Cargill, would you order scones and cakes if you were about to be executed for your past sins?\" [2224] \"No, I would be too nervous to eat or drink.\" [2225] \"Perhaps our three executioners thought they needed to conserve their energy for the upcoming task,\" Poirot speculated. \"However, when the food arrived, they couldn't eat it. But where did the food go?\" [2226] \"Are you asking me?\" Jennie said, \"I don't know, because I wasn't there.\" [2227] \"Let's go back to the time of the murder,\" Poirot said. \"The pathologist determined that the three deaths occurred between four and eight-thirty. Later, indirect evidence narrowed the time of death to seven-fifteen to eight-ten. Alright, let's look at these indirect evidence. Server Rafael Bocar was in Room 317 at seven-fifteen and saw the three victims, and then Thomas Braynell met Richard Nigges in the hotel lobby at seven-thirty, where Nigges praised Braynell's efficiency and asked him to ensure the tea point charges were on his account, and ordered a sherry. So, none of the cases happened before seven-fifteen, and Richard Nigges couldn't have died before seven-thirty. [2228] \"However, there are still many details that don't fit together, and we can't form a complete picture. First, we know that Harriet Sipper, Adela Granbriery, and Richard Nigges didn't eat those pastries, but they disappeared. I don't believe that a person who is about to commit their first murder would want to eat a scone before doing so. Since they didn't plan to eat, why did they order them? It can only be to create an alibi, to prove that they were still alive at seven-fifteen. But why make people think that the three victims were still alive at that time? According to Jennie Hobbs, I can only think of one explanation: if our conspirators knew, for whatever reason, that Nancy Duane had no alibi between seven-fifteen and eight-fifteen, then they had to ensure that the deaths occurred within that time frame. But Nancy Duane's alibi was very solid, wasn't it, Mrs. Wallace?\" [2229] Louisa Wallace stood