nd talk to her.' At that point, the man in the room ignored you and the food and said, 'Oh, Harriet, that's not fair. Ada can be easily frightened, be kind to her.' Do I have it right, Mr. Burbank?\" [2247] \"Yes, sir.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 8:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n\"If the killer wanted to move the victim's body out of the hotel room, he wouldn't care about the width of the door.\" [1572] Poirot shook his head sadly and muttered under his breath. [1573] He turned to ask Razuri. \"Sir, tell me everything you know, from the beginning.\" [1574] \"Of course, certainly,\" Razuri cleared his throat. \"The guest staying in this room was named Jenny Hobbs. Mr. Poirot, she looked like she was in a hurry, rushed into the hotel, threw money on the front desk, and demanded a room, as if she was running away from a demon chasing her! I personally brought her to the room and wondered what to do next? Should I call the police and say that a woman named Jenny had checked into the hotel? Mr. Poirot, you specifically asked me about this name, but there are countless Jennys in London, not just one sad and troubled woman, and her reason for being sad may not be related to murder. How could I know she\u2014\"\n[1575] \"Sir, please get to the point,\" Poirot interrupted him. \"Then what did you do?\" [1576] \"I waited for about thirty minutes and then went up to the fifth floor, knocked on the door. But no one answered! So, I went back down to get the key.\" [1577] While Lazari was talking, I walked over to the window and looked outside. Any view was more pleasant than the bloodstain, the hat, and the dreadful monogrammed cufflink. The room faced the hotel garden, just like Richard Nigges' room on the second and third floors. I stared at the vast lawn but soon looked away because it felt like they were staring back at me: an endl