che and sat down in an armchair, starting to talk incessantly about the conversations he had with Rafael Borgia, Samuel Kid, Nancy Duane, and Louisa Wallace while I was in Grelling. By the time he finished, I was dizzy, so I took a risk and encouraged him to continue. \"Did you leave out any important details?\" [1632] \"Such as?\" [1633] \"Oh, the useless and clumsy maid of Louisa Wallace's house - Dolores. You emphasized that when you stood on the landing with her, you suddenly realized something important, but you didn't say what it was.\" [1634] \"Indeed, I didn't.\" [1635] \"And the mysterious painting, you said it was sent to Scotland Yard after it was finished. What was it about? What was Stanly Bill going to do with it?\" [1636] \"Ah, I didn't tell you that either.\" Poirot seemed a bit apologetic, as if he had no choice. [1637] I foolishly asked, \"And why do you want to know how many times each employee at the Bluxton Hotel has seen Richard Nix, Harriet Sipper, and Ada Gransby, whether alive or dead? What's the point of that? You didn't explain that either.\" [1638] \"Poirot, you left so many blanks!\" [1639] \"Don't forget the earlier questions you left unanswered. For instance, the murder at the Bluxton Hotel and the strange outburst by Jenny Hobbs at the 'Joyful Cafe' both had two extremely unusual characteristics. You also mentioned that they had two extremely unusual common points.\" [1640] \"I did say that, my friend, but I wanted you to become a detective.\" [1641] \"This case can only make me a pitiful, useless man,\" I said, revealing my true feelings for the first time, \"and that's the most maddening thing.\" [1642] Suddenly, I heard a sound, like someone knocking on the door of the living room, or not. \"Who is it?\" I shouted. [1643] \"It's me,\" said Blanche Enderby's voice from the hall, sounding somewhat anxious, \"gentlemen, I'm sorry to disturb you at this time, but a lady is looking for M. Poirot. She said she couldn't wait.\" [1644] \"Please let her in, madam.\" [1645] A few seconds later, I found myself in the presence of the artist Nancy Drew. I felt that most men would find her alluringly beautiful. [1646] Poirot introduced us politely. [1647] \"Thank you for seeing me,\" Nancy Drew's eyes were puffy, indicating that she had been crying, and her expensive-looking dark green coat looked expensive, \"I feel very presumptuous to disturb you like this, but...you see, I still came. I tried to convince myself not to come, but...\"\n[1648] \"Please sit down, Mrs. Drew,\" Poirot said, \"How did you find us?\" [1649] \"Like a proper detective, I found out where you lived from Scotland Yard,\" Nancy managed a slight smile. [1650] \"Oh! Poirot, he thought he had chosen a place where no one could find him, but the police brought the guests to the door! No matter, madam, I'm glad to see you, just a little surprised.\" [1651] \"I want to tell you what happened sixteen years ago in Glebeholme,\" said Miss Duane. \"I should have told you earlier, but when you mentioned those names I never wanted to hear again, I was really scared.\" [1652] She unbuttoned her coat and took it off, gesturing for her to sit down. [1653] After sitting down, she said, \"It's a sad story.\" [1654] Miss Duane spoke in a low voice, her eyes betraying her nervousness. The story she told us was exactly the same as Margaret Ernst had told me in Glebeholme, about how Patrick Ives was cruelly defamed. When she mentioned Jenny Hobbs, her voice even trembled. \"She was the worst. You see, she fell in love with Patrick. Oh, there was no evidence, but that's how I think it. What she did to him showed her love for him: because of jealousy, she fabricated an unforgivable lie. He loved me, so she wanted to hurt him, punish him. Then, when Harriet caught her in the lie, Jenny realized how much damage she had caused and felt sorry. I believe she must have been terribly ashamed and hated herself, but she didn't take any steps to make amends, she didn't do anything! She ran away, hid herself, hoping no one would ever find her. Even though she was so afraid of Harriet, she should have been brave enough to stand up and say, 'I told a lie, I'm sorry.'\" [1655] \"I'm sorry, madam. You said there was no evidence that Jenny loved Patrick Ives, so I would like to ask, how do you know she loved him? As you said, a person who loves him would never defame him like that, it's hard to believe.\" [1656] \"I just believe she loved Patrick without a doubt,\" Nancy insisted. \"She left her lover in Cambridge and moved to Gresham Holling with Patrick and Francis. Do you know about that?\" [1657] We all shook our heads. [1658] \"They were supposed to get married. I believe they even had a date set, but Jenny couldn't let go of Patrick, so she canceled the wedding and left with him.\" [1659] \"Perhaps the one she couldn't let go of was Francis Yve, or the Yves themselves? She might have valued loyalty over romantic love.\" [1660] \"I don't believe any woman would be so loyal to her employer that she would give up her own marriage,\" said Nancy. [1661] \"I don't believe it either, madam. But what you say is not very logical. If Jenny was jealous of love, why did she only tell this lie when Patrick Yve fell in love with you? Why didn't she feel jealous when he married Francis?\" [1662] \"How do you know she wasn't jealous? Patrick and Francis met and got married in Cambridge. Jenny Hobbs served him then. Maybe she whispered some nasty things in his friend's ear, but that friend wasn't Harriet Sipper, and the mean words didn't spread.\" [1663] Poirot nodded, saying, \"You are right, there is a possibility.\" [1664] \"Most people don't like to spread bad rumors, thank goodness,\" Nancy said, \"In Cambridge, there might not have been anyone as malicious as Harriet Sipper, and no one as zealous in promoting morality and piety as Ada Gransbury.\" [1665] \"I noticed you didn't mention Richard Nigg.\" [1666] Nancy looked somewhat confused. \"Richard is a good man. He regretted what he did in this terrible affair later. Oh, when he found out it was Jenny's vile lie, he was deeply sorry and stopped sympathizing with Ada. A few years ago, he wrote me a letter from Devonshire, saying this matter had been tormenting his heart. He said Patrick and I shouldn't have done what we did, and he always believed that marriage vows were marriage vows, but he began to think that punishment was not always the right way to solve problems, even if someone had done wrong.\" [1667] \"He said that in his letter?\" [1668] \"Yes. I suppose you don't agree.\" [1669] \"These matters are complicated, madam.\" [1670] \"For instance, is it right to commit a greater sin to punish someone for loving the wrong person, and to cause two deaths, one of which was innocent?\" [1671] \"Yes, that's exactly the kind of dilemma that makes things so complicated.\" [1672] \"He also wrote in the letter that, as a Christian, he didn't believe God would want him to persecute someone as naturally good as Patrick.\" [1673] \"Punishment and persecution are two different concepts.\" Poirot said, \"There's another question: was there any illegal activity involved in the whole affair? Falling in love...after all, we can't control our feelings, but we can choose whether to act on them.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n\"Mrs. Duane is one of our most famous artistic talents and a loyal guest of our hotel. You must have made a mistake!\" [2304] \"I have not made a mistake, my friend.\" [2305] I looked at Nancy Duane, who sat quietly, without getting up to deny what Poirot had said. [2306] Famous artist Nancy Drew, plotting with the ex-fianc\u00e9 of Janice Hobbs? I had never been so confused in my life. What was going on here? [2307] \"Katchurpul, didn't I tell you? Madame Drew is wearing a scarf today because she doesn't want to be recognized. You thought I meant 'doesn't want to be recognized as a famous portrait painter.' No, she doesn't want to be recognized by Raffles Burbach. She was the one who disguised herself as Harriet in Room 317 that night!\" [2308] Nancy removed her scarf. [2309] \"Mr. Burbach, is it this lady you saw?\" [2310] \"Yes, M. Poirot. It's her.\" [2311] The restaurant was silent, and all that could be heard was the sound of people taking deep breaths and holding their breath. The large restaurant was eerily quiet. [2312] \"How could you not recognize her as the famous portrait painter Nancy Drew?\" [2313] \"No, sir. I know nothing about art, I've only seen her introduction in the newspaper. And at the time, she turned her face away.\" [2314] \"I believe she turned her face away to avoid the possibility that you might be an art lover and recognize her.\" [2315] \"I recognized her as soon as she entered today, and that man Kid. I wanted to tell you, but you didn't let me speak.\" [2316] \"Yes, Thomas Brine also wanted to tell me that he recognized Samuel Kid,\" said Poirot. [2317] \"I can't believe two of the people I thought were dead walked into the restaurant alive!\" exclaimed Raphael Barker's voice, still not recovered from the shock. [2318] \"What about the alibis provided by Sir Walter and Lady Wallace?\" I asked. [2319] \"That evidence was false,\" said Nancy. \"It's my fault, please don't blame them. They are my good friends who wanted to help me. Saint John and Louisa had no idea I was at the Bluxton Hotel on the night of the incident. I swore to them that I wasn't there, and they believed me. They are good people, brave people, unwilling to see me falsely accused of murder. Mr. Poirot, I believe you already know everything. So you must also know that I didn't kill anyone.\" [2320] \"Lying to a police officer investigating a murder case is not brave, madam. It's unforgivable. Lady Wallace, I knew you were lying when I left your house.\" [2321] \"How dare you speak to my wife like that?\" said Sir John Wallace. [2322] \"If the truth is too much for you to bear, I apologize, Sir John,\" said Poirot. [2323] \"How did you know, Mr. Poirot?\" asked his wife. [2324] \"You hired a new maid, Dorcas. I asked you to bring her here today because she is important. You told me that Dorcas had only been at your house for a few days, and I saw that she was a bit clumsy. She brought me a cup of coffee, but she spilled almost all of it. Fortunately, there was still some left, and I drank it all. I immediately tasted that it was coffee from 'The Happy Coffee House.' The taste is unique and different from any other place.\" [2325] \"Oh my!\" exclaimed Phyllis Spence. [2326] \"Yes, madam. I remember that coffee very well, so I put all the scattered details together. High-concentration coffee is very beneficial to the brain.\" [2327] \"This clumsy maid - sorry, Dulcinites, I believe you will improve with time - is new! I combined this situation with the coffee from 'The Happy Coffee House' and had an idea: could Dulcinites have been the maid of Lady Wallace before? I learned from the waitress at 'The Happy Coffee House' that Jane Hobbs used to buy things for her employer there. Her employer was a fashionable lady of society, and Jane often referred to her as 'Lady Sir.' Jane was serving her mistress a few days ago who was providing an alibi for Nancy Drew, isn't that interesting? This could be an incredible coincidence - or it could be completely different! At first, my thoughts were off track, and I thought 'Nancy Drew and Lady Wallace, the two friends, were planning to kill poor Jane.'\" [2328] \"You're crazy!\" exclaimed Lady Wallace. [2329] \"Shocking lies!\" echoed her husband, John. [2330] \"Not lies, not at all, just a mistake. Everyone saw that Jane didn't die. But I was right, she was a maid at Sir John and Lady Wallace's house, and she was recently replaced by Dulcinites. After our conversation at 'The Happy Coffee House' that night, Jane had to leave the Wallace house very quickly. She knew I would soon come to verify Nancy Drew's alibi. If I had seen her serving the lady who was providing our alibi, I would have immediately suspected something. Captain, tell everyone, what would I suspect?\" [2331] I took a deep breath, hoping I wasn't mistaken. \"You would suspect Jean Hobbs and Nancy Drew conspired to deceive us,\" I said. [2332] \"Absolutely correct, my friend.\" Poirot smiled at me, then turned to the audience, \"Before I drank the coffee and recognized it as from 'The Happy Coffee House', I had been looking at Mr. St. John Wallace's painting, a wedding anniversary gift to his wife. It depicted blue morning glories, with the date of August 4th last year, when Wallace Lady commented on it. Then I realized something else \u2013 the portrait of Lady Wallace that Nancy Drew had given her didn't have a date. As an art enthusiast, I have attended numerous art exhibitions in London and have seen many of Mrs. Drew's works. Her paintings always have a date in the lower right corner, along with her initials 'NAED'.\" [2333] \"You're much more attentive than other visitors,\" Nancy said. [2334] \"Hercule Poirot is always meticulous. Lady, I believe you marked the date on Lady Wallace's portrait but later erased it. Why? Because it wasn't a new work. You wanted me to believe that the painting was given to Wallace Lady on the night of the murder, that it was a recently completed portrait. I asked myself, why didn't you put a false date on it? The answer is obvious \u2013 if your works were passed down to posterity, art historians would be very interested in them, and you didn't want to mislead them, or those who care about your works. Yes, you only wanted to mislead Hercule Poirot and the police!\" [2335] Nancy Drew tilted her head, pondering, \"You really have a keen eye, Mr. Poirot. You really know everything, don't you?\" [2336] \"Yes, madam. I also know that you helped Jeanne Hobbs find a job as a maid at the Wallace house. She needed a job when she came to London, and you helped her. I also know that Jeanne Hobbs never really intended to participate in the plot to frame you, Richard Nix. In fact, in Glebeholme, Jeanne Hobbs and Nancy Drew were friends and allies. These two women, who love Patrick Yves unconditionally, conspired to create a clever plan that almost fooled even Hercule Poirot and the police\u2014but not quite clever enough!\" [2337] \"Lies, all lies!\" Jeanne Hobbs cried. [2338] Nancy remained silent. [2339] Poirot continued, \"Let's return to the Wallace household. I studied Nancy Drew's portrait of Lady Wallace for a long time and noticed a set of blue pots and bowls. As I walked around the room, I observed it from different angles and under different lighting conditions, and I found that no matter how I looked at it, these pots and bowls were just dull, monotonous color blocks. But the other colors in the painting changed with my perspective and the light. Nancy Drew is an experienced painter, especially in coloring, a genius\u2014unless she was too rushed to consider the artistic aspect and only thought about protecting herself and her friend Jeanne Hobbs. She hastily painted the originally non-blue pots and bowls blue to cover up information. Why did she do this?\" [2340] \"To cover up the date?\" I said. [2341] \"No. The jugs and bowls were located in the upper part of the portrait, and Nancy Drew habitually placed the date in the bottom right corner of her paintings.\" [2342] \"But why would she paint the jugs and bowls blue?\" Poirot asked. \"I believe she wanted to hide the motif. Motifs are not just decorative, like family crests or emblems of famous universities.\" [2343] \"Cambridge University, Jesus College,\" I blurted out the words without thinking. I remembered Stanley Bill mentioning a badge when Poirot and I were preparing to leave London for Glydehurst. [2344] \"Yes, Carrington. After leaving the Wallace household, I sketched the motif to prevent forgetting. Although I am not an artist, I managed to draw it. Then, I asked Inspector Bill to investigate its origins. As you all heard, my friend Carrington said that the motif on the white jugs and bowls in the Wallace household belonged to Jesus College at Cambridge University. That's where Jenny Hobbs used to work, as a maid for Patrick Ivory the vicar. Miss Hobbs, was that the gift you took with you when you left Jesus College? You went with Patrick and Frances Ivory to Glydehurst, and you brought it with you when you moved to the Wallace knight's home, but you didn't take the jugs and bowls with you when you hurriedly hid at Mr. Kid's house \u2013 you simply didn't have time to think about such things then. I believe that after you left, Miss Wallace took the jugs and bowls from your former servant's room and moved them to a guest room, perhaps to please the guests.\" [2345] \"I did not!\" Dolly seemed shaken. \"I've never seen blue jugs and bowls in the house!\" [2346] Jenny didn't respond, her face pale and expressionless. [2347] Poirot said: \"Nancy Drew didn't want to take any risks. She knew that after the hotel murder case, Kitchup and I would go to Gleghorn village to investigate. What if Walter Sturkey, the former teacher of Jesus College, told us that he had given Jenny Hobbs a set of pot and bowl with embellishments as a farewell gift? If we saw the same embellishment on Lady Wallace's portrait, we might discover a connection between her and Jenny Hobbs, and then find that their relationship was not as envious and enemies as the outside world thought, but rather friends or accomplices. Lady Wallace couldn't take the risk of letting us see the embellishment on the portrait, so she painted the white set of pot and bowl blue \u2013 hastily and roughly.\" [2348] \"No one can be a masterpiece every time, Poirot sir.\" Nancy said. Her counterargument sounded reasonable, and what surprised me even more was that, facing the accusation of conspiring to commit three murders, she still spoke politely and rationally. [2349] \"Mr. Wallace, do you agree with Lady Wallace's explanation?\" Poirot said, \"You are also an artist, although your styles differ. Ladies and gentlemen, Saint John Wallace is a botanical painter. When I visited his house, I found that every room had his works. Lady Wallace generously showed me around her house, just as generously she provided a false alibi for Nancy Drew to protect her. You can see that Lady Wallace is a good person. The most dangerous good person, who cannot see evil even when it is in front of her!\" [2350] \"How did you know?\" Saint John Wallace asked. [2351] \"Luckily guessed, sir. When Mrs. Dunn asked you for these plants, she said it was for art, right? And you believed her.\" [2352] \"Now, Mr. Poirot, I didn't kill anyone,\" said Mrs. Dunn. \"I believe you know I'm telling the truth. Please explain to everyone here that I'm not the killer.\" [2353] \"I can't do that, madam. I'm sorry. Although you didn't personally poison them, you were involved in this conspiracy and ended the lives of three people.\" [2354] \"Yes, but it was to save another,\" said Mrs. Dunn sincerely. \"I didn't commit a crime! Come over, Jenny, and let him hear our story - the real story. He will admit that we only did what was necessary, just to save our own lives.\" [2355] The restaurant was silent, and everyone sat there quietly. I thought Jenny wouldn't go, but she eventually stood up slowly, holding her handbag tightly in her arms, and walked towards Mrs. Dunn. She said, \"Our lives are not worth saving.\" [2356] \"Jane!\" Samuel Kid called out, also suddenly getting up from his chair and rushing towards her. I watched him, feeling a strange sensation. Time seemed to have stopped. Why was Kid running? Was there danger? It was clear that he knew there was danger, and for some reason, my heart began to beat wildly. Something terrible was about to happen, and I began to run towards Jane. [2357] She opened her handbag and said to Mrs. Dunn, \"You wanted to reunite with Patrick, didn't you?\" It felt like her voice, but not like her voice. Every word she spoke seemed to be wrapped in darkness, and I hoped it would be the last time I ever heard such a voice. [2358] Poirot began to walk towards them, but we were both too far away. \"Poirot!\" I shouted. \"Who will stop her?\" I saw the glint of metal, followed by a flash of light. The two men sitting next to Mrs. Dunn also stood up, but their movements were too slow. \"No!\" I cried out. Jane's hand moved quickly, and suddenly blood gushed out, flowing down Mrs. Dunn's skirt onto the floor. A woman somewhere in the back of the restaurant also let out a piercing scream. [2359] Poirot stopped in his tracks, standing there dumbfounded. \"Oh, my God!\" he said, closing his eyes. [2360] Samuel Kid reached Mrs. Dunn's side before I did. \"She's dead,\" he said, looking down at the corpse on the ground. [2361] \"Yes, she's dead,\" Jenny said. \"I stabbed her in the heart, right through the heart.\" [2362] Chapter 25\n[2363] If the murder started with D\n[2364] That day, I finally realized I wasn't afraid of death. It was a state of no energy, devoid of power. I had seen many corpses during my work, and they had never unsettled me too much. Yes, what I feared most was the state of a living person approaching death: Jenny Hobbs' voice, consumed by the desire to kill; the mindset of the killer, calculatingly placing the initial-lettered cufflinks into the victim's mouth, meticulously arranging the body, with straight limbs and fingers, lifeless hands, palm down. [2365] \"Hold his hand, Edward.\" [2366] How could anyone not be afraid of being pulled towards death when holding the hand of a dead person? [2367] If possible, I wouldn't let any living person deal with death. Although I knew it was an unrealistic wish. [2368] After Jenny Hobbs stabbed Nancy, I no longer wanted to approach her. I was not curious about why she did it; I just wanted to go home, sit by the fireplace with Blanche Onsworth, and forget about the murder at the Brocks Han hotel. Or the letter cufflink murder, whatever you want to call it. [2369] However, Pollock's willpower was clearly stronger than mine. He insisted on my staying, saying it was my case and must be handled properly before leaving. He made a gesture with his hands, as if wrapping something up, as if investigating a murder case was wrapping a package. [2370] So, a few hours later, I found myself sitting in a small room at Scotland Yard, across the table from Jenny Hobbs. Samuel Kid was also arrested and being interrogated by Stanley Bill. If it were Kid, I would do everything in my power to expose this fraud and unquestionably bad man, and he still hasn't given up until now. [2371] As for the tone of voice, I was startled by Poirot's gentle voice when he spoke: \"Lady, why did you do it? Why did you kill a friend and ally for so long?\" [2372] \"Nancy and Patrick truly loved each other, both in life and death. I just found out today, and I had no idea before. I always thought she and I both loved Patrick, knew we couldn't be together, and never did. I always thought their love was pure, but it wasn't. If Nancy really loved Patrick, she shouldn't have had an affair with him, tarnishing his character.\" [2373] Jeanne wiped away her tears and continued, \"I was helping her. You heard her, how much she wanted to be with Patrick. I helped her, didn't I?\" [2374] \"Carter, when I told you we couldn't save Jeanne lady after finding the bloodstains in room 402 at the Brawley Hotel, do you remember what I said?\" [2375] \"I remember.\" [2376] \"You thought I meant she was dead, but you misunderstood. You see, I knew at that moment that Miss Janice was beyond saving. I was concerned that she had done something terrible that required her to pay with her life, that was what I meant.\" [2377] \"Either way, I died when Patrick did,\" said Janice, her tone filled with despair. [2378] I knew that the only way to get through this torture was to focus all my attention on the logic of the events. Had Poirot solved the mystery? It seemed so, but I was still confused. Who killed Harriet Spear, Ada Gransbury, and Richard Nix, and why? I asked these questions again. [2379] \"Ah.\" He smiled gently, as if I had reminded him of a joke we both knew, \"My friend, I know what you're struggling with. You've listened to Poirot for so long, just when he was about to reveal the answer, another murder was committed, and you still haven't heard the answer you've been waiting for. It's a shame.\" [2380] \"Please tell me now, so the shame can end,\" I said forcefully. [2381] \"It's simple, Jenny Hobbs and Nancy Duane, with the help of Samuel Kid, conspired to kill Harriet Westphal, Ada Gransbury, and Richard Nix.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nIf you ask a doctor or a medical expert, theoretically, can a twelve or thirteen-year-old girl have a child? The answer is either 'yes' or 'no.' Let's not get caught up in the age at which a woman can have a child! Remember Samuel Kid's comment about the young man, it was quite amusing. He said, 'Does he have a brain? I don't think he does.' You would think that Kid's meaning was that the young man was a fool.\" [2441] \"Yes, that's exactly what I think,\" I said impatiently. \"Since you're so much smarter than me, why don't you just tell me where I'm wrong?\" [2442] Polo grumbled discontentedly. \"My goodness! The couple in Room 317 were Harriet Sipper and her husband George. It wasn't a serious debate, it was just joking. George Sipper died when they were both young, and Samuel Kid said he didn't have a brain, meaning that if he existed after death, he wouldn't be in human form, but in a soul, right? Souls don't have human brains, so George Sipper's soul couldn't have a brain either.\" [2443] \"I\u2026 oh, my goodness. Yes, now I understand.\" [2444] \"Samuel Kid began his argument with 'I think\u2026' because he knew that Nancy Duane would disagree with him. She might say, 'Souls certainly have ideas, and souls can be attached to people, don't they have free will? Where does their will come from if they don't have a brain?'\" [2445] From a philosophical perspective, this is an interesting point of view. In other circumstances, I might also express my own opinion.\" [2446] Poirot continued, \"Nancy said 'old enough to be his mother' because she believed that when a person dies, their age is fixed forever. In the afterlife, they won't age. If George Sipper's soul could come back to visit his wife, he would still be a twenty-something-year-old, the same age he was when he died. And she, as a forty-something-year-old woman, is now old enough to be his mother.\" [2447] \"Great!\" Jenny exclaimed sincerely, \"I wasn't there at the time, but they argued again in front of me later. Mr. Poirot, you are truly insightful. Mr. Kitchener, I hope you appreciate him.\" She then turned to Poirot and said, \"Their argument never ends...oh, forever! Nancy insists she's right, and Sem doesn't back down. He says souls are not limited by time dimensions, they don't have a concept of time, so you can't say someone is old enough to be a soul's mother.\" [2448] Poirot said to me, \"How disgusting, isn't it, Mr. Kitchener? When Rafael Buccar went to deliver the dessert, Nancy Drew sat next to the corpse of Ida Gransberry propped up on a chair, mocking another woman who died that day due to their conspiracy. Poor and foolish Harryette, her husband had no interest in communicating with her directly from the grave, he only wanted to talk to Jenny Hobbs. And Harryette had no choice, if she wanted to receive his message, she had to go to Bluxton to see Jenny. But going to see Jenny meant certain death.\" [2449] \"No one deserves to be killed more than Harryette Sipper.\" Jenny said, \"I regret many things, but I don't regret killing Harryette.\" [2450] \"What about Ida Gransberry?\" I asked, \"Why did she go to the Bluxton Hotel?\" [2451] \"Ah!\" Poirot said, always eager to share his seemingly endless knowledge with others, \"Ada also received an irresistible invitation from Richard Nigges. Not to speak with the deceased lover, but to meet her separated for sixteen years' ex-fianc\u00e9. It's not hard to imagine how tempting that is. Richard Nigges abandoned Ada, which undoubtedly broke her heart. She remained single for life. I think he hinted at reconciliation, even possibly marriage, in his letter. Ada agreed \u2013 who wouldn't give love another chance? Richard told her he would come to her room at Bluxton Hotel on Thursday from 3:30 to 4:00 pm. Ketteral, do you remember saying that you would go to the hotel on Wednesday so that you could spend Thursday preparing for the murder? Does it make more sense now?\" [2452] I nodded and said, \"Nigges knew he was going to commit murder on Thursday and that he would be killed himself. To prepare mentally for the double test, he naturally wanted to be on the scene a day earlier.\" [2453] \"Also to avoid any delays that could hinder the plan,\" Poirot said. [2454] \"So, Jane Hobbs killed Harriet Seppel, and Richard Nigges killed Ada Gransberry?\" I asked. [2455] \"Yes, my friend,\" Poirot said, looking at Jane, who nodded. \"I believe the murders in rooms 121 and 317 were carried out around the same time. I suspect the methods were the same, luring Harriet and Ada to drink poison. Jane told Harriet, 'I think you need to drink some water before I tell you what I have to say. Sit down, and I'll get you some water.' They took cups from the sink to fill with water, and they slipped in the poison. Then they handed the poisoned water to the victims, who soon died.\" [2456] \"How did Richard Naygreaves die?\" I asked. [2457] \"According to their previous plan, Jeanne was supposed to kill him,\" said Poirot. [2458] \"Most of what I said to you at Semple's was true,\" said Jeanne. \"Richard did write to me after many years. He was tormented by what he had done to Patrick and Francis. He couldn't find a way out, couldn't make amends, couldn't find inner peace, unless we all paid with our lives. We all had a responsibility.\" [2459] \"He made you... help him kill Harriet and Ida?\" I asked, trying to clarify the situation. [2460] \"Yes, they, and myself,\" said Jeanne. \"He insisted that we all had to die, otherwise it wouldn't have any meaning. He didn't want to be a murderer, he wanted to be an executor - he used that word several times, which means that neither of us could escape punishment. I agreed that Harriet and Ida should die, they were too evil. But... I didn't want to die, nor did I want Richard to die. I think what he did was sincere repentance for what he had done, and that was enough for Patrick, and for any higher power that might exist or not exist. But I couldn't convince Richard. I soon realized that there was no point in trying to convince him. He was as clever as ever, but he had lost some parts that made him paranoid, strange, and come up with many absurd ideas. He never forgot that event, those sins... He became a strange madman. I knew that if I didn't comply with his proposal, he would kill me. He didn't say it outright because he didn't want me to be coerced. You know, he was very good to me, he just needed an ally, someone who shared his views. He sincerely believed that I would agree to his plot because he thought I was more reasonable than Harriet and Ida. He was too confident in his own opinion, believing that only by sacrificing our four lives could he atone for his sins. I think he may have been right, but I was afraid. But now I'm not afraid anymore, and I don't know what changed me. Maybe, despite living in pain all these years, I still held a glimmer of hope that things would get better. Sadness does not equal despair.\" [2461] \"You knew you had to pretend to comply to save your life,\" said Poirot. \"Perfectly deceiving Richard Nix was the only way to escape death. But you didn't know how, so you turned to Nancy Drew.\" [2462] \"Yes, I went to her. She helped me, or so I thought. Her plan was brilliant. She suggested that I change one part of the plan. Richard's original plan was that once Harriet and Ada were dead, he would kill me and then commit suicide. Naturally, as someone who enjoyed being in control and being the authority, he wanted to be in charge from start to finish. [2463] \"Nancy told me to convince Richard that I should kill him, not the other way around. 'Impossible!' I said, 'He won't agree.' But Nancy said he would, as long as I presented it the right way. She advised me to act more obsessed with our goal than he was. She was right; it worked. I went to Richard and told him that it wasn't enough for just the four of us\u2014me, him, Harriet, and Ada\u2014to die. Nancy had to be punished too. I told him that Nancy was even more evil than Harriet, and how she had plotted to steal Patrick away from his wife and wouldn't stop until she succeeded. I also told him that Nancy had confessed to me that her speech at the King's Head Inn wasn't meant to help Patrick, but to hurt Francis. Nancy wanted Francis to commit suicide or at least give up Patrick and return to Cambridge to be with her father, so she could take his place.\" [2464] \"More lies,\" said Poirot. [2465] \"Yes, more lies\u2014but these were Nancy's ideas, and they worked! Richard agreed that I should die first.\" [2466] \"And he didn't know about Samuel Kid, did he?\" said Poirot. [2467] \"No, he didn't. Nancy and I brought in Sem, who was part of our plan. Neither Nancy nor I wanted to climb out the window, down the tree, and risk breaking our necks after locking the door and hiding the key behind the tiles. But that was the only way out of Room 238, so we needed Samuel's help. He not only did that but also impersonated Richard.\" [2468] \"Hiding the key behind the tiles,\" I murmured to myself, confirming the details in my mind. \"So, the story you told us at Mr. Kid's house was seamless, Richard Naylors hiding the key to make us think the killer took it, so he could frame Nancy Drew for the murder at the Blaux Han hotel.\" [2469] \"That's right,\" said Poirot. \"More precisely, he thought that was the case. When Jenny gave him the poisoned water, he still believed Jenny would live and ensure that Nancy Drew was accused of the murder at the Blaux Han Hotel. He believed Jenny would report to the police and make sure they suspected Nancy. But he didn't know that Nancy had prepared a solid alibi, that she was with the Wallace couple at that time! He also didn't know that a brooch would be placed in his mouth, that the key would be hidden behind the tiles, and that the windows would be open...\"\n[2470] \"Yes, Richard didn't know all of that,\" agreed Jenny. \"You now understand why I said Nancy's plan was brilliant, Mr. Poirot.\" [2471] \"She's a talented artist, madam. A clever artist who can see how to design details and also grasp the framework, knowing how to integrate all the elements together.\" [2472] Jenny turned to me and said, \"Nancy and I didn't want to do this, you have to believe me, Mr. Ketchup. If I had opposed Mr. Richard's plan, he would have killed me.\" She sighed. \"We planned it all, Nancy would escape unharmed, and Nancy and I would be punished for framing and accusing her, but not with the death penalty. We hoped to serve a few months in prison. After that, I planned to marry Sammy.\" [2473] \"Madam, everything was already ruined. I know you killed Harriet Sipper and Richard Nigges.\" [2474] \"I didn't kill Richard, Mr. Poirot. You're wrong about that.\" [2475] \"Yes, but you deceived him. Richard Nigges agreed to die because you agreed to participate in his plan, that all four of you would die. Then you brought Nancy Dunn into it, and the plan became five people. But you didn't really agree to his plan. You betrayed him and set up another trap behind his back. Who knows if Richard Nigges would have chosen to die in that way if he had known about your secret agreement with Nancy Dunn?\" [2476] Jane's expression stiffened. \"I didn't kill Richard Nigg. I killed him in self-defense, or he would have killed me.\" [2477] \"You said he didn't explicitly say he would kill you.\" [2478] \"No, but I knew he would. What do you think, Mr. Cattermole? Did I kill Richard Nigg?\" [2479] \"I don't know.\" [2480] \"Cattermole, my friend, don't be silly.\" [2481] \"He's not silly.\" Jinnie said. \"He's using the part of his brain you don't want to use, Mr. Poirot. Please, just think again, please. I would like to hear you say I didn't murder Richard Nigg before I'm hanged.\" [2482] I stood up and said: \"Let's go, Poirot. I wanted to end this conversation while the echo of 'hope' still lingered in the room.\" [2483] Epilogue\n[2484] Four days later, I sat by the fireplace in Mrs. Blanche Onsworth's house, sipping a glass of brandy and engrossed in a crossword puzzle when Poirot entered the living room and stood silently beside me for several minutes. I didn't look up at him. [2485] Finally, he cleared his throat and said: \"Caderpillar, you still won't talk to me about how Richard Nigg died - whether he was murdered, committed suicide with assistance, or killed in self-defense?\" [2486] \"I don't see the point in arguing about it,\" I said, feeling a spasm in my stomach. I no longer wanted to discuss the murder at the Baskerville Hotel. What I wanted to do, or needed to do, was to write it all down, to put the whole story on paper in detail. Was there such a difference between saying something and writing it down? [2487] \"Don't be afraid, my friend,\" said Poirot. \"I won't mention it again. We can talk about something else, like how I went to the 'Jolly Coffee Shop' this morning. Miss Spence asked me to tell you that she'd like to talk to you when you're free. She's a bit upset.\" [2488] \"With me?\" [2489] \"Yes. She said she was just hearing about the case at the Baskerville Hotel one moment, and the next, it was all over. A murder happened right in front of everyone, and yet no one saw the end. Miss Spence wants you to tell her the whole story from beginning to end.\" [2490] \"It wasn't my fault that murder happened,\" I whispered. \"Why can't she read about it in the newspaper like everyone else?\" [2491] \"No, she wants to talk to you alone. As a waitress, she's quite intelligent and a rare woman. Don't you think so, my friend?\" [2492] \"I know what you're up to, Poirot,\" I said weakly. \"Just drop it, stop wasting time, and tell Phyllida... sorry... you see, I'm busy. Can you leave?\" [2493] \"You're angry with me.\" [2494] \"Yes, a bit,\" I admitted. \"Henry Nayes and the suitcase, Raffles Burbank and the laundry cart, Thomas Brine and his girlfriend in the hotel garden, who was wearing a light brown coat, half of England wears such coats. And the handcart\u2026\"\n[2495] \"Ah!\" [2496] \"Yes, 'Ah'. You know that Jeanne Hobby isn't dead, yet you deliberately mislead me, making me suspect that her body was moved from Room 402, in the most impractical ways?\" [2497] \"My friend, because I wanted to encourage you to use your imagination. If you don't consider the most unlikely possibilities, you can't become the best detective. I'm teaching you to use your grey cells, forcing them to move in a different direction, so that you can be inspired.\" [2498] \"Do as you please.\" [2499] \"You must think that Poirot is going too far, beyond what is necessary. Maybe so.\" [2500] \"In Room 402, you made a fuss over those bloodstains, the direction of the bloodstains, the width of the door... What does it all mean? You know that Jeanne Hobby wasn't killed and wasn't dragged anywhere.\" [2501] \"I know, but you don't. You and our friend Mr. Lazari both believe that Jeanie is dead and that the blood on the floor belongs to her. Oh, I want you to think for yourself: a suitcase, a wheeled laundry cart - these two tools can directly enter Room 402 and be placed next to the body. So why did the killer drag the body towards the door? He couldn't have done that! Therefore, she couldn't have been dead! The bloodstains dragged towards the door were a deception, intended to make us believe that the body was moved elsewhere. This detail was done very convincingly, so it was very important as evidence at the crime scene. [2502] \"But for Hercule Poirot, it was just a small detail that allowed him to verify his suspicions. Jeanie Hobbs was not killed in that room, and no one was killed. Because I can't think of a reason to drag a bloody body to the door. No killer would walk around the hotel's public corridor with a victim's body, unless they put it in a container. But I can think of containers that can easily be brought into the room and directly put the body in and take it away, without dragging the body to the door. Caderpillar, this logic is very simple. I'm surprised you didn't think of it immediately.\" [2503] \"It's simple for you.\" I said, \"Next time, if you want me to think of something immediately, just tell me directly. Whatever it is, be direct, and it will save a lot of trouble.\" [2504] He smiled. \"Okay. I will learn to be more direct from my good friend Caderpillar. I will start learning now!\" [2505] \"If by 'private affairs' you mean Phyllis Spoon, I can tell you that we are impossible, forever impossible.\" I glanced at the letter in his hand. \"What about the private affairs of another pitiful person? Why thank you?\" [2506] Poirot smiled. \"Dr. Ambrose Flavard and his wife.\u201d He handed me the letter. [2507] \"Who came to the letter?\u201d\n[2508] Poirot smiled. \"Ambrose Flavard, M.D., and his wife.\u201d He handed me the letter.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."